Chapter: Console

 We say:

Section: Definitions

  effect Console where
    print-line-uni : Text -> [Console.Write] Nothing
    read-line  : [Console.Read] Text

Page 1

Chapter: DeviceMath

 We say:

Section: Clamps and Selection

  real-min : Real, Real -> Real
  real-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  real-max : Real, Real -> Real
  real-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  real-abs : Real -> Real
  real-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

Section: Square Root

  real-sqrt : Real -> Real
  real-sqrt (x) =
    if x <= 0.0 then 0.0
    else dm-sqrt-scaled x 1.0 700

  dm-sqrt-scaled : Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  dm-sqrt-scaled (x) (s) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then s * dm-sqrt-core x
    else if x >= 4.0 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x / 4.0) (s * 2.0) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.25 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x * 4.0) (s * 0.5) (fuel - 1)
    else s * dm-sqrt-core x

  dm-sqrt-core : Real -> Real
  dm-sqrt-core (r) =
    let g0 = (r + 1.0) * 0.5
    in let g1 = (g0 + r / g0) * 0.5
    in let g2 = (g1 + r / g1) * 0.5
    in let g3 = (g2 + r / g2) * 0.5
    in let g4 = (g3 + r / g3) * 0.5
    in (g4 + r / g4) * 0.5

Section: Sine and Cosine

  dm-two-pi : Real
  dm-two-pi = 6.283185307179586

  dm-pi : Real
  dm-pi = 3.141592653589793

  dm-half-pi : Real
  dm-half-pi = 1.570796326794897

  dm-reduce : Real -> Real
  dm-reduce (x) =
    let k = real-from-int (real-to-int (x / dm-two-pi))
    in let r = x - k * dm-two-pi
    in if r > dm-pi then r - dm-two-pi
       else if r < 0.0 - dm-pi then r + dm-two-pi
       else r

  dm-fold-quadrant : Real -> Real
  dm-fold-quadrant (r) =
    if r > dm-half-pi then dm-pi - r
    else if r < 0.0 - dm-half-pi then 0.0 - dm-pi - r
    else r

  dm-sin-poly : Real -> Real
  dm-sin-poly (r) =
    let r2 = r * r
    in let r3 = r2 * r
    in let r5 = r3 * r2
    in let r7 = r5 * r2
    in let r9 = r7 * r2
    in let r11 = r9 * r2
    in r - r3 / 6.0 + r5 / 120.0 - r7 / 5040.0 + r9 / 362880.0 - r11 / 39916800.0

  real-sin : Real -> Real
  real-sin (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce x))

  real-cos : Real -> Real
  real-cos (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce (x + dm-half-pi)))

Chapter: Num

 The Real primitives Codex's foreword does not have.

 `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` is the only Real chapter in the foreword and its whole
 surface is min, max, abs, sqrt, sin and cos. There is no rounding, no floor and
 no modulo, and the foreword's other numeric chapters -- Cordic, Easing, Color,
 GameCamera, Numeric -- are all INTEGER milli-units, so they answer a different
 question. Everything here is a gap upstream rather than a hole in the zig plug,
 and unlike `Safari chapter Trig` none of it is a stand-in for something that
 already exists.

 EVERY FUNCTION HERE NEEDS real-to-int, so none of it could have been written
 before 2026-08-29 -- see PORTING_NOTES A1 and plugs-backlog row 2.03. That is
 the honest reason this chapter did not exist earlier: the port had no way to
 reach the integers at all.

 We say:

Section: Rounding

 Zig's @round goes to nearest and ties AWAY FROM ZERO, which truncation does not
 give. TRUNCATE FIRST AND THEN LOOK AT WHAT IS LEFT: real-to-int already truncates
 toward zero, `x - t` is exact for every x this converter accepts, and a leftover
 of half or more in either direction is precisely the away-from-zero tie. One rule
 covers both signs, which is why there is no sign branch here any more.

 ADDING THE HALF FIRST IS THE VERSION THAT WAS WRONG, and NumCheck is what found
 it. `real-to-int (x + 0.5)` answers 1 for x = 0.49999999999999994 -- the largest
 double below a half -- because x + 0.5 is not representable and rounds UP to
 exactly 1.0, and the mirrored form answered -1 at -0.49999999999999994 where zig
 answers -0. Every caller in the game rounds a 0..255 colour channel, a stride
 count, a step count or a whole number of degrees, so not one of them could see
 it; a round that is wrong on a value none of today's callers produces is a trap
 for the next caller, and that is the whole reason this chapter is graded on a
 domain wider than its callers'.

  round-real : Real -> Real
  round-real (x) =
    let t = real-from-int (real-to-int x)
    in let f = x - t
    in if f >= 0.5 then t + 1.0 else if f <= 0.0 - 0.5 then t - 1.0 else t

 real-to-int truncates toward zero, so for a negative non-integral value it lands
 ABOVE its argument and floor is one less. An exact integer is its own floor in
 both arms, which is why the test is `>` and not `>=`.

  floor-real : Real -> Real
  floor-real (x) =
    let t = real-from-int (real-to-int x)
    in if t > x then t - 1.0 else t

Section: Modulo

 Zig's @mod is the FLOORED modulo -- its result takes the sign of the divisor --
 and that is not the truncated remainder. Floored is x - m * floor (x / m), and
 this is the faithful reading of what tower.zig asks for.

 BUT THE ONE CALLER CANNOT TELL THE DIFFERENCE, and that is worth stating rather
 than implying coverage this does not have. tower.zig writes

     @mod(@mod(phase, P) + P, P)

 which looks like belt and braces over an operator that is already non-negative,
 and is in fact exactly what makes the two semantics AGREE. Floored gives m in
 [0, P) directly and the second pass is a no-op. Truncated gives r in (-P, P);
 adding P lands in (0, 2P); the second pass takes P off again when it overflowed
 and leaves it alone when it did not -- and r + P is precisely floored's answer
 whenever r was negative. So the double form is semantics-agnostic by
 construction.

 MEASURED, not reasoned about after the fact: replacing this with the truncated
 remainder leaves TowerCheck entirely green, including its negative-phase case.
 An earlier draft of this note claimed tower.zig "relies on" the floored form and
 that the two "disagree there". Both were wrong, and the red test said so.

 Floored stays because it is what @mod means and the next caller may not wrap its
 argument twice. But nothing in this port grades the choice today.

  mod-real : Real, Real -> Real
  mod-real (x) (m) = x - m * floor-real (x / m)

Section: The Exponential

 rider.zig's shoulder brake decays as exp(-n / 20) over up to 2000 frames, so the
 argument reaches -100 and a bare Taylor series is useless there. The standard
 reduction: take k as the nearest integer to x / ln 2, so the remainder r is
 within half a ln 2 of zero, sum the series there, and scale by 2^k.

 THIS NEEDS real-to-int TWICE OVER -- once to round k, once to count the doublings
 -- so like everything else here it could not have been written before the
 conversion emitters landed.

  ln2 : Real
  ln2 = 0.6931471805599453

 TWELVE TERMS OVER |r| <= 0.347 LEAVE A REMAINDER OF 1.7e-16, NOT THE 1e-17 THIS
 NOTE CLAIMED BEFORE IT WAS MEASURED. r^13/13! at r = ln2/2 is 1.671e-16, which is
 an arithmetic fact nobody had worked out. NumCheck now measures the whole chapter
 end to end against zig's @exp over eighteen arguments: the worst RELATIVE error is
 8.5e-16, at x = 20, and the gate is 1e-15 because the stream reddens at 8e-16.

 AND IT IS THE REDUCTION, NOT THE SERIES, THAT SETS THAT NUMBER. The three
 arguments small enough to need no reduction at all -- -0.05, 0 and 0.05 -- come
 out EXACT; every nonzero error in the table belongs to a reduced case. Taking
 k * ln2 off x cancels, and `ln2` here is one f64 where a library keeps a hi/lo
 pair for exactly this reason. Splitting it is the fix if it ever matters:
 rider.zig consumes the answer in f32, where 8.5e-16 is nine orders below the last
 bit it can hold, so today it does not.

 WRITING A BOUND NOBODY CHECKED IS THE DEFECT THIS PORT IS SENDING UPSTREAM.
 FINDINGS.md item 2 is `Math chapter Cordic` claiming ~0.1% and delivering 0.45%,
 with a test that never calls a Cordic function. The claim above was 17x out for
 the same reason -- it was reasoned about rather than run -- and it is corrected
 here rather than left standing while we mail someone else's.

  exp-poly : Real -> Real
  exp-poly (r) =
    let t1 = r
    in let t2 = t1 * r / 2.0
    in let t3 = t2 * r / 3.0
    in let t4 = t3 * r / 4.0
    in let t5 = t4 * r / 5.0
    in let t6 = t5 * r / 6.0
    in let t7 = t6 * r / 7.0
    in let t8 = t7 * r / 8.0
    in let t9 = t8 * r / 9.0
    in let t10 = t9 * r / 10.0
    in let t11 = t10 * r / 11.0
    in let t12 = t11 * r / 12.0
    in 1.0 + t1 + t2 + t3 + t4 + t5 + t6 + t7 + t8 + t9 + t10 + t11 + t12

 2^k by repeated multiplication rather than by assembling an exponent field: the
 bit-level route would need real-to-bits, which this arm still has no emitter for,
 and k is small enough here that the loop is exact anyway -- every step multiplies
 by a power of two, which is exact in binary until it underflows.

  pow2-up : Integer, Real -> Real
  pow2-up (k) (acc) = if k <= 0 then acc else pow2-up (k - 1) (acc * 2.0)

  pow2-down : Integer, Real -> Real
  pow2-down (k) (acc) = if k >= 0 then acc else pow2-down (k + 1) (acc * 0.5)

  pow2-int : Integer -> Real
  pow2-int (k) = if k >= 0 then pow2-up k 1.0 else pow2-down k 1.0

  exp-real : Real -> Real
  exp-real (x) =
    let k = real-to-int (round-real (x / ln2))
    in exp-poly (x - real-from-int k * ln2) * pow2-int k

Chapter: Trig
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath

 Sine, cosine, tangent and arc tangent in Real.

 THE STAND-IN IS RETIRED. This chapter used to carry its own sine and cosine --
 the same reduction, quadrant fold and 11th-order Taylor polynomial DeviceMath
 has -- because DeviceMath's dm-reduce calls real-from-int and the zig plug had no
 emitter for it. That emitter landed (PLUG_WORK, 2026-08-29), so `r-sin` and
 `r-cos` are now DeviceMath's, and the duplicate implementation is gone.

 THE NAMES STAY AND THE IMPLEMENTATION DOES NOT, which is the whole change. Nine
 chapters call `r-sin`, `r-cos` or `r-tan`, and none of them should have to know
 whether the sine underneath is the foreword's or the port's -- that was the point
 of the names. What they buy now is one place to look and one place that already
 cites DeviceMath, rather than nine new cites for a rename.

 RETIRING IT MOVED NUMBERS, and that was the reason it waited rather than being
 tidied away with the rest. The old `wrap` subtracted two-pi in a bounded loop;
 dm-reduce divides and converts back through the integers, so the two disagree in
 the last bits and every angle in the game runs through one of them. See the
 tolerance note in README and D10.

 We say:

Section: Constants

  pi : Real
  pi = 3.141592653589793

  two-pi : Real
  two-pi = 6.283185307179586

  half-pi : Real
  half-pi = 1.570796326794897

  deg : Real
  deg = 0.017453292519943295

Section: Angle Wrapping

  wrap : Real, Integer -> Real
  wrap (x) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then x
    else if x > pi then wrap (x - two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.0 - pi then wrap (x + two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else x

Section: Sine and Cosine

 `wrap` OUTLIVED THE SINE IT WAS WRITTEN FOR, and it turns out not to have been
 part of the stand-in at all. mountains.zig and sky.zig each carry their own
 `wrap` -- normalising a bearing difference into the half-open turn around zero --
 and both are ported through this one. It reads as an angle utility now rather
 than as an argument reduction, which is what those two callers always wanted;
 what it is NOT any more is anything sine depends on.

 The fuel is the zig's own shape: mountains.zig's wrap is a bounded `while`, not a
 modulo, for exactly the same reason.

  r-sin : Real -> Real
  r-sin (x) = real-sin x

  r-cos : Real -> Real
  r-cos (x) = real-cos x

 DeviceMath has no tangent, so this one is still the port's -- and it is the
 obvious quotient rather than anything clever, exactly as it was.

  r-tan : Real -> Real
  r-tan (x) = real-sin x / real-cos x

Section: Arc Tangent

 THIS ONE IS NOT A STAND-IN. `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` has no atan at all -- its
 whole surface is min, max, abs, sqrt, sin and cos -- and the foreword's other
 arc tangents (Math chapter Cordic, Math chapter Geodesic) are INTEGER milli-unit
 routines, one of which is recorded in PORTING_NOTES A4 as four times less
 accurate than it claims. So a Real atan is a gap in Codex's foreword rather than
 a hole in the zig plug, and it does not go away when the rest of this chapter is
 retired in favour of DeviceMath.

 The method is argument HALVING, not a Taylor series applied directly: the series
 for atan converges hopelessly slowly near |t| = 1, which is exactly where the
 mountains sample it. The half-angle identity

     atan t = 2 * atan (t / (1 + sqrt (1 + t*t)))

 shrinks the argument geometrically, so four applications take |t| <= 1 down to
 about 0.06, where a six-term series is good to roughly 1e-14 and the sixteen it
 is multiplied back by cannot undo that. Above |t| = 1 the reciprocal identity
 folds it back into range first; the screen's outer columns at a pulled-in focal
 do reach |t| near 3, so that branch is live and not defensive.

  atan-half-step : Real -> Real
  atan-half-step (t) = t / (1.0 + real-sqrt (1.0 + t * t))

  atan-halve : Real, Integer -> Real
  atan-halve (t) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then t
    else atan-halve (atan-half-step t) (n - 1)

  atan-series : Real -> Real
  atan-series (u) =
    let u2 = u * u
    in let u3 = u2 * u
    in let u5 = u3 * u2
    in let u7 = u5 * u2
    in let u9 = u7 * u2
    in let u11 = u9 * u2
    in u - u3 / 3.0 + u5 / 5.0 - u7 / 7.0 + u9 / 9.0 - u11 / 11.0

 Four halvings, so the series result is scaled back by 2^4.

  atan-core : Real -> Real
  atan-core (t) = 16.0 * atan-series (atan-halve t 4)

  r-atan : Real -> Real
  r-atan (t) =
    if t > 1.0 then half-pi - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else if t < 0.0 - 1.0 then (0.0 - half-pi) - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else atan-core t

 The TWO-argument arc tangent, which is not a convenience wrapper: it is the one
 that knows which quadrant the point is in, and gaze.zig needs that because the
 pig it is aiming at can be behind the rider as well as ahead. Dividing first and
 calling r-atan would fold two quadrants onto one.

 The x = 0 arms are exact rather than a limit, and the y = 0, x = 0 case answers
 zero, which is what std.math.atan2 does with two positive zeros.

  r-atan2 : Real, Real -> Real
  r-atan2 (y) (x) =
    if x > 0.0 then r-atan (y / x)
    else if x < 0.0 then (if y >= 0.0 then r-atan (y / x) + pi else r-atan (y / x) - pi)
    else if y > 0.0 then half-pi
    else if y < 0.0 then 0.0 - half-pi
    else 0.0

 Zig's std.math.sign, which answers zero AT zero rather than picking a side.

  r-sign : Real -> Real
  r-sign (x) = if x > 0.0 then 1.0 else (if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else 0.0)

Chapter: Cat
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Num

 The cat's placement AND its crossing state.

 A partial port of wasm/cat.zig: the `Cat` record, `CAT_ALONG` and `make`. That is
 what world.zig needs to author a segment, and it is arithmetic over five
 constants with no dependencies at all.

 `draw` IS STILL DEFERRED AND THE REASON IS NOT DIFFICULTY. It reaches
 `cat_frames.zig`, which is 434 KB of BAKED polygons -- and baked means GENERATED,
 by ops/bake_cat running the real TypeScript drawing code through a recorder.
 NOTES section 5 scopes that out and says how it should arrive when it does: teach
 the baker a Codex emitter and the data ports itself. Hand-porting 434 KB of
 generated coordinates would be the wrong shape of work and would rot the moment
 the baker ran again.

 But `state` is NOT drawing, and it is ported below. It picks which still to flip
 to, how far across the cat has walked or leapt, and how high the hop is -- and
 the pose it returns is an INDEX, a plain integer, not a polygon. Nothing in it
 reaches the baked tables. So the cat can be placed, clocked and depth-sorted; it
 just has no art yet, like every other animal on the route.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

  cat-height : Real
  cat-height = 1.7

 The desired spot down the road. world.zig rounds it up to just past a tree, so a
 tree stands between the rider and the cat.

  cat-along : Real
  cat-along = 105.0

 Clearance beyond the roadside tree line, and how far past the rounding tree it
 sits.

  cat-road-gap : Real
  cat-road-gap = 1.5

  cat-beyond-tree : Real
  cat-beyond-tree = 2.0

 Head-centre x in the unit frame, from the cat's anatomy.

  cat-head-x : Real
  cat-head-x = 0.0 - 0.48

 The hind paw's reach in the landing pose, and how far past the left edge it
 settles.

  land-hind-reach : Real
  land-hind-reach = 0.70 * cat-height

  grass-toehold : Real
  grass-toehold = 0.3

Section: Placement

  Cat = record {
    along : Real,
    start-across : Real,
    mid-across : Real,
    end-across : Real,
    height : Real
  }

 The waiting spot is right of the road; mid-across is the anchor offset that puts
 the head centre on the lane; end-across puts the hind paw barely onto the left
 grass.

  cat-make : Real, Real, Real -> Cat
  cat-make (lane-half) (tree-offset) (tree-along) =
    let tree-x = lane-half + tree-offset
    in Cat {
      along = tree-along + cat-beyond-tree,
      start-across = tree-x + cat-road-gap,
      mid-across = (0.0 - cat-head-x) * cat-height,
      end-across = (0.0 - (lane-half + grass-toehold)) - land-hind-reach,
      height = cat-height
    }

Section: The Crossing Window

 The cat's crossing takes this many frames end to end: ten to enter the road,
 twenty-four frozen in the headlight, and four to escape -- coil, airborne twice,
 land, collapse. The rider's throttle gate reads it, which is why these three are
 here while the poses they name are not.

  enters-road-steps : Real
  enters-road-steps = 10.0

  frozen-steps : Real
  frozen-steps = 24.0

  escapes-steps : Real
  escapes-steps = 4.0

  cross-frames : Real
  cross-frames = enters-road-steps + frozen-steps + escapes-steps

 Metres of clear road kept between rider and cat.

  road-buffer : Real
  road-buffer = 3.0

 Is the cat still ahead, beyond the buffer, AND inside the crossing window -- that
 is, crossing in front of us right now? The rider's accel gate holds the throttle
 while this holds: he stops CLOSING on the cat but never brakes.

  cat-in-danger : Real, Real -> Boolean
  cat-in-danger (gap-along) (v) =
    let e = gap-along - road-buffer
    in if e > 0.0 then e <= cross-frames * v else False


Section: The Crossing Clock

 Progress 0..1 from the rider's along-gap to the cat and his speed. The gap is
 measured to a point road-buffer short of the cat, so he is clear with road still
 to spare. A pure function of the clock, so it freezes on pause and scrubs on
 reverse.

  clamp01 : Real -> Real
  clamp01 (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 else (if x > 1.0 then 1.0 else x)

  lerp : Real, Real, Real -> Real
  lerp (a) (b) (t) = a + (b - a) * t

  cross-t : Real, Real -> Real
  cross-t (gap) (v) =
    let e = gap - road-buffer
    in if e <= 0.0 then 1.0
      else if v <= 0.000001 then 0.0
      else clamp01 (1.0 - e / (cross-frames * v))

 THE RIDER'S ATTENTION ON THIS CAT, which is the one thing cat.zig computes that
 is not about drawing or placing it. It feeds `camera.camFocal`: the lens pulls in
 -- narrows -- as he watches the crossing, peaks at an exaggerated 1.8 as the cat
 lands, and eases back over eighty-three frames once he is level with the landing
 point. View only; nothing about the ride changes.

 THE TWO HALVES RUN ON DIFFERENT CLOCKS and that is the whole shape of it. Ramping
 UP is the crossing's own progress, so it tracks the cat; ramping DOWN is metres
 behind him over his speed, so it tracks the RIDER. Reading the second as a
 crossing progress would hold the lens narrow for as long as the cat took to
 cross, which is not what a person's attention does.

  focus-peak : Real
  focus-peak = 1.8

  focus-ramp-down : Real
  focus-ramp-down = 83.0

 Its own smoothstep rather than Gaze's, and the duplication is forced: World cites
 this chapter to place a cat on every segment, and Gaze cites World, so citing
 Gaze from here is a cycle. One flat namespace also means the two cannot share a
 name (PORTING_NOTES B5).

  cat-smoothstep : Real -> Real
  cat-smoothstep (t) = t * t * (3.0 - 2.0 * t)

  cat-focus : Real, Real -> Real
  cat-focus (gap-along) (v) =
    if v <= 0.000001 then 0.0
    else if gap-along - road-buffer > 0.0 then cat-smoothstep (cross-t gap-along v) * focus-peak
    else cat-focus-past (gap-along - road-buffer) v

 Past the landing point: `e` is negative, so `0 - e` is how far behind him it is.

  cat-focus-past : Real, Real -> Real
  cat-focus-past (e) (v) =
    (1.0 - cat-smoothstep (real-min ((0.0 - e) / v / focus-ramp-down) 1.0)) * focus-peak

 Gait phase over a walk of `phase-len` steps: a whole number of leg cycles, so the
 legs start and end at rest.

  stride-steps : Real
  stride-steps = 5.0

  gait : Real, Real -> Real
  gait (p) (phase-len) =
    let c = round-real (phase-len / stride-steps)
    in p * (if c < 1.0 then 1.0 else c) * two-pi

Section: The Stills

 Pose INDICES into cat_frames, which are plain integers. The frames themselves are
 not ported; these say WHICH one, and that is the part this chapter owns.

  pose-rest : Integer
  pose-rest = 0

  pose-stride : Integer
  pose-stride = 1

  pose-frozen : Integer
  pose-frozen = 2

  pose-coil : Integer
  pose-coil = 3

  pose-flight : Integer
  pose-flight = 4

  pose-land : Integer
  pose-land = 5

  pose-collapse : Integer
  pose-collapse = 6

  leap-pose-for : Real -> Integer
  leap-pose-for (t) =
    if t < 0.20 then pose-coil
    else if t < 0.70 then pose-flight
    else if t < 0.95 then pose-land
    else pose-collapse

  leap-height : Real
  leap-height = 0.18

Section: The Cat's State

 Which still, how far across, and how high. Three phases: he walks in to the lane
 centre, freezes in the headlight, then leaps clear.

  CatState = record {
    pose-idx : Integer,
    across : Real,
    lift : Real
  }

 THE AIRBORNE LATERAL IS AN APPROXIMATION AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE IN THIS PORT.

 cat.zig front-loads the leap with `pow(b, 0.7)`. It is the only `pow` in the
 whole game -- the sole other transcendental outside the trig is rider.zig's
 `@exp`, which Num ports -- so the missing piece is a LOGARITHM, since pow is exp
 of a log, and the foreword has none. Rather than write one for a single call
 site, this uses `b^0.75`, which needs no logarithm at all: 0.75 is DYADIC where
 0.7 is not, so it is `sqrt(b * sqrt(b))` and square roots both languages already
 have. Verified exact to 1.1e-16 against b^0.75.

 THE COST IS BOUNDED AND MEASURED: |b^0.7 - b^0.75| peaks at 0.0254 near b = 0.27,
 and it is zero at both ends of the leap, so the cat launches and lands in exactly
 the right places and is at most 0.0254 of the crossing span off in between. The
 span is 4.306 m -- mid-across 0.816 to end-across -3.490 -- so the bound is
 10.9 cm, for two frames, on a cat being driven past. Steve's call: the game
 is not changed to suit the port, and the port does not need to be picture-perfect
 to be useful. CatCheck grades this one seam at a gate sized to that bound and
 says so; every other value here is graded at the ordinary width tolerance.

  pow075 : Real -> Real
  pow075 (b) = real-sqrt (b * real-sqrt b)

  cat-state : Cat, Real, Real -> CatState
  cat-state (c) (gap-along) (v) =
    let step = cross-t gap-along v * cross-frames
    in let escape-at = enters-road-steps + frozen-steps
    in if step <= enters-road-steps then cat-entering c step
      else if step <= escape-at then CatState { pose-idx = pose-frozen, across = c.mid-across, lift = 0.0 }
      else cat-escaping c (step - escape-at)

 ENTERING: he walks in to the lane centre, and the still snaps between rest and a
 single mid-stride on the LIFTED half of the gait. The zig's `> 0` on the sine is
 a plain comparison, not an approximate one, so it stays exact here.

  cat-entering : Cat, Real -> CatState
  cat-entering (c) (step) =
    let p = if enters-road-steps > 0.0 then step / enters-road-steps else 1.0
    in CatState {
      pose-idx = if r-sin (gait p enters-road-steps) > 0.0 then pose-stride else pose-rest,
      across = lerp c.start-across c.mid-across p,
      lift = 0.0
    }

 ESCAPING, by whole escape-steps: under one he is coiled and still planted; one to
 three he is airborne over two frames; past three he is down on the far side,
 landing then collapsing.

  cat-escaping : Cat, Real -> CatState
  cat-escaping (c) (raw-k) =
    let k = if raw-k > escapes-steps then escapes-steps else raw-k
    in let pose = leap-pose-for (k / escapes-steps)
    in if k < 1.0 then CatState { pose-idx = pose, across = c.mid-across, lift = 0.0 }
      else if k < 3.0 then cat-airborne c pose ((k - 1.0) / 2.0)
      else CatState { pose-idx = pose, across = c.end-across, lift = 0.0 }

 b runs 0 at launch to 1 at touchdown. The lift is a low parabola -- zero, peak,
 zero -- and is plain arithmetic; only the lateral needs the front-loading.

  cat-airborne : Cat, Integer, Real -> CatState
  cat-airborne (c) (pose) (b) =
    CatState {
      pose-idx = pose,
      across = lerp c.mid-across c.end-across (pow075 b),
      lift = leap-height * 4.0 * b * (1.0 - b)
    }

Chapter: World
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The road network as data: a chain of straight segments joined by turns. Each
 segment carries its length, width, tree rows, cow herd, pigs, and its EXIT turn --
 angle, direction, and the segment it leads to -- and build-world accumulates each
 segment's heading relative to north.

 A port of wasm/world.zig.

 NO RIDER, NO PROJECTION, NO DRAWING. This is the first chapter in the port that
 draws nothing at all: it is authored data plus the deterministic generators that
 expand it. That makes it seam 2 in NOTES' terms -- the seam that compares
 EXACTLY, because there is no arithmetic here whose width could differ except the
 jitter sines and the turn angles.

 The route is the full nineteen segments of the TypeScript. The baseline is
 deliberately boring -- all-green trees and a cow herd -- so the rare departures
 surprise, and novelty is front-loaded: the cat debuts on segment 2, pigs on 3,
 gold trees on 4. Segment 19 terminates; the rider arrives and stops.

 PIGS AND SAFARI CREATURES ARE AUTHORED HERE BUT THE CRITTER DRAWING IS NOT
 PORTED -- critter.zig needs emoji_frames.zig, 182 KB of generated bakes that
 NOTES scopes out. The placement data is still ported because it is the world's,
 not the renderer's, and because it is what the exit-corner logic reads.

 We say:

Section: Palette and Dimensions

  conifer-green : Integer
  conifer-green = 1858082

  conifer-gold : Integer
  conifer-gold = 13605400

  conifer-red : Integer
  conifer-red = 11680298

  small-height : Real
  small-height = 4.5

  big-scale : Real
  big-scale = 1.3

 Metres between trees, and this is FIXED where the TypeScript fixes the COUNT --
 so density, and the speed illusion it creates, is uniform across segments.

  tree-spacing : Real
  tree-spacing = 30.0

  tree-road-offset : Real
  tree-road-offset = 1.5

  tree-start-inset : Real
  tree-start-inset = 6.0

  tree-end-inset : Real
  tree-end-inset = 85.0

  lane-width : Real
  lane-width = 4.0

 Longer segments stand their own tower halfway down.

  mid-tower-min-length : Real
  mid-tower-min-length = 1000.0

  max-trees : Integer
  max-trees = 96

Section: Schemes and Creatures

  Scheme =
   | AllGreen
   | YellowGreen
   | RedGreen

 The big animal parked at a segment's EXIT corner. Elephant is the unmarked
 default; the rest are the rare departures. DuckPond is a hand-drawn scene rather
 than an emoji pair, and NoCreature is the terminus, which has no exit corner.

 The constructor is DuckPond rather than Pond because chapters share one flat
 namespace inside a bundle (CDX3001) and `Safari chapter Pond` is already in this
 port -- a collision that would only appear once something cited both.

  Creature =
   | NoCreature
   | Elephant
   | Giraffe
   | Zebra
   | Rhino
   | DuckPond

Section: Trees and Critters

  Tree = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    color : Integer,
    height : Real
  }

 A roadside animal: an emoji billboard at along and across-from-centre.

  Critter = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    codepoint : Integer,
    height : Real,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

  accent-color : Scheme -> Integer
  accent-color (s) =
   when s
    is YellowGreen -> conifer-gold
    is RedGreen -> conifer-red
    is AllGreen -> conifer-green

 Conifers every tree-spacing metres from the start inset to the end-clear zone.
 Even index is green and 1.3 times tall, odd is the scheme's accent. Red trees are
 twice everything; gold three times and set about four times further off the road.

 The pair is emitted together, so the cap is checked two at a time exactly as the
 zig's `n_trees + 2 <= MAX_TREES` does.

  tree-height-for : Integer, Boolean -> Real
  tree-height-for (color) (even) =
    let base = if even then small-height * big-scale else small-height
    in if color == conifer-red then base * 2.0 else (if color == conifer-gold then base * 3.0 else base)

  tree-x-for : Integer -> Real
  tree-x-for (color) =
    if color == conifer-gold then lane-width / 2.0 + 4.0 * tree-road-offset
    else lane-width / 2.0 + tree-road-offset

  fill-trees : Scheme, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List Tree
  fill-trees (scheme) (length) (along) (k) (n) =
    if along > length - tree-end-inset then []
    else if n + 2 > max-trees then []
    else fill-trees-pair scheme length along k n

  fill-trees-pair : Scheme, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List Tree
  fill-trees-pair (scheme) (length) (along) (k) (n) =
    let even = k - ((k / 2) * 2) == 0
    in let color = if even then conifer-green else accent-color scheme
    in let height = tree-height-for color even
    in let x = tree-x-for color
    in [Tree { along = along, across = 0.0 - x, color = color, height = height }, Tree { along = along, across = x, color = color, height = height }] & fill-trees scheme length (along + tree-spacing) (k + 1) (n + 2)

Section: The Cow Herd

 The boring CONSTANT on every segment, near the start and on the left: a bull
 leading ten cows and four calves in a deterministic jittered grid. No randomness
 anywhere -- the jitter is a sine of the index.

  bull-cp : Integer
  bull-cp = 128002

  cow-cp : Integer
  cow-cp = 128004

  cow-height : Real
  cow-height = 1.4

  calf-height : Real
  calf-height = cow-height / 2.0

  bull-height : Real
  bull-height = cow-height * 1.15

  herd-road-offset : Real
  herd-road-offset = 10.0

  bull-dist : Real
  bull-dist = 24.0

  bull-tree-gap : Real
  bull-tree-gap = 0.5

  herd-gap-behind-bull : Real
  herd-gap-behind-bull = 6.0

  herd-col-spacing : Real
  herd-col-spacing = 6.0

  herd-row-stagger : Real
  herd-row-stagger = 2.0

  herd-row-depth : Real
  herd-row-depth = 5.0

  herd-jitter-along : Real
  herd-jitter-along = 1.5

  herd-jitter-across : Real
  herd-jitter-across = 1.2

 The bull is authored per segment in the route table, like the cat and the pigs,
 so the whole schedule of surprises reads in one place.

  bull-of : Boolean -> List Critter
  bull-of (bull) =
    if bull then [Critter { along = bull-dist, across = 0.0 - (lane-width / 2.0 + tree-road-offset + bull-height / 2.0 + bull-tree-gap), codepoint = bull-cp, height = bull-height, face-right = False }]
    else []

  cows-from : Integer -> List Critter
  cows-from (i) =
    if i >= 14 then []
    else [cow-at i] & cows-from (i + 1)

  cow-at : Integer -> Critter
  cow-at (i) =
    let fi = real-from-int i
    in let col = real-from-int (i / 3)
    in let row = real-from-int (i - ((i / 3) * 3))
    in let along = bull-dist + herd-gap-behind-bull + col * herd-col-spacing + (row - 1.0) * herd-row-stagger + herd-jitter-along * r-sin (fi * 2.7)
    in let across = 0.0 - (lane-width / 2.0 + herd-road-offset + row * herd-row-depth + herd-jitter-across * r-cos (fi * 1.9))
    in Critter { along = along, across = across, codepoint = cow-cp, height = if i - ((i / 4) * 4) == 1 then calf-height else cow-height, face-right = True }

  fill-cows : Boolean -> List Critter
  fill-cows (bull) = (bull-of bull) & cows-from 0

Section: The Pigs

 The DISTRACTION herd is a deep seven-by-seven staggered block near the segment's
 end, on the right, and the rider gawks at one designated pig as he passes. Only
 the first two pig appearances distract -- the novelty wears off.

  pig-cp : Integer
  pig-cp = 128022

  pig-height : Real
  pig-height = 1.1

  pig-novelty-count : Integer
  pig-novelty-count = 2

  pig-dist-before-end : Real
  pig-dist-before-end = 60.0

  gaze-pig-along-offset : Real
  gaze-pig-along-offset = 2.0

  big-herd-cols : Integer
  big-herd-cols = 7

  big-herd-rows : Integer
  big-herd-rows = 7

  pig-col-spacing : Real
  pig-col-spacing = 4.0

 Across-spacing AND the per-row along-shear, which is what slants the herd's sides
 at forty-five degrees.

  pig-row-depth : Real
  pig-row-depth = 6.0

  pig-jitter-along : Real
  pig-jitter-along = 1.2

  pig-jitter-across : Real
  pig-jitter-across = 1.0

  pig-herd-first-col : Real
  pig-herd-first-col = 0.0 - 6.0

  pig-back-row-offset : Real
  pig-back-row-offset = 6.0

 The ONE front-row pig the distracted rider fixes on, so the head-turn always aims
 at a pig that is actually drawn.

  gaze-pig : Real, Real -> Critter
  gaze-pig (length) (lane-half) =
    Critter { along = length - pig-dist-before-end + gaze-pig-along-offset, across = lane-half + herd-road-offset, codepoint = pig-cp, height = pig-height, face-right = False }

  herd-pig-at : Real, Integer, Integer -> Critter
  herd-pig-at (base) (r) (c) =
    let i = real-from-int (r * big-herd-cols + c)
    in let fr = real-from-int r
    in let fc = real-from-int c
    in let along = base + pig-herd-first-col + fc * pig-col-spacing + fr * pig-row-depth + pig-jitter-along * r-sin (i * 2.3)
    in let across = lane-width / 2.0 + herd-road-offset + fr * pig-row-depth + pig-jitter-across * r-cos (i * 1.7)
    in Critter { along = along, across = across, codepoint = pig-cp, height = pig-height, face-right = False }

  herd-cols-from : Real, Integer, Integer -> List Critter
  herd-cols-from (base) (r) (c) =
    if c >= big-herd-cols then []
    else [herd-pig-at base r c] & herd-cols-from base r (c + 1)

  herd-rows-from : Real, Integer -> List Critter
  herd-rows-from (base) (r) =
    if r >= big-herd-rows then []
    else (herd-cols-from base r 0) & herd-rows-from base (r + 1)

  fill-pig-herd : Real -> List Critter
  fill-pig-herd (length) = herd-rows-from (length - pig-dist-before-end) 0

 The small non-distraction row: a front rank of four at the road edge and a back
 rank of six further off. The rider does not gawk at these.

  pig-row-front : List Real
  pig-row-front = [0.0 - 6.0, 0.0 - 2.0, 2.0, 6.0]

  pig-row-back : List Real
  pig-row-back = [0.0 - 10.0, 0.0 - 6.0, 0.0 - 2.0, 2.0, 6.0, 10.0]

  row-pigs-at : Real, Real, List Real, Integer -> List Critter
  row-pigs-at (base) (across) (ds) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ds then []
    else [Critter { along = base + list-at ds i, across = across, codepoint = pig-cp, height = pig-height, face-right = False }] & row-pigs-at base across ds (i + 1)

  fill-pig-row : Real -> List Critter
  fill-pig-row (length) =
    let base = length - pig-dist-before-end
    in let edge = lane-width / 2.0 + herd-road-offset
    in (row-pigs-at base edge pig-row-front 0) & row-pigs-at base (edge + pig-back-row-offset) pig-row-back 0

Section: The Route

  Cfg = record {
    length : Real,
    scheme : Scheme,
    turn-deg : Real,
    cat : Boolean,
    pigs : Boolean,
    bull : Boolean,
    terminates : Boolean,
    creature : Creature
  }

 turn-deg is the SIGNED exit turn onto the next segment, positive right and
 negative left. The LAST entry has no turn: it is the terminus, the photo-finish
 line.

  cfg : Real, Scheme, Real, Boolean, Boolean, Boolean, Boolean, Creature -> Cfg
  cfg (len) (sch) (turn) (c) (p) (b) (t) (cr) =
    Cfg { length = len, scheme = sch, turn-deg = turn, cat = c, pigs = p, bull = b, terminates = t, creature = cr }

  route : List Cfg
  route = [cfg 500.0 AllGreen 50.0 False False False False Elephant, cfg 320.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) True False False False Elephant, cfg 400.0 AllGreen 20.0 False True False False Elephant, cfg 300.0 YellowGreen 20.0 False False False False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) False False True False Giraffe, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) False False True False Elephant, cfg 1200.0 RedGreen 80.0 False False True False Rhino, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 15.0 False False True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) False False True False Elephant, cfg 800.0 AllGreen 15.0 False False True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 15.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 15.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 RedGreen 15.0 True True True False DuckPond, cfg 400.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 50.0) False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 50.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 RedGreen (0.0 - 50.0) False False True False Zebra, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 50.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 50.0) False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 RedGreen 0.0 True True True True NoCreature]

Section: Building

 The smallest right-side tree `along` at or after `desired` -- the cat tucks just
 past it, so a tree stands between the rider and the cat. The zig starts at
 infinity and takes the minimum; this threads a Boolean instead, because Codex has
 no infinity literal and a sentinel is a worse answer than saying what is meant.

  next-tree-loop : List Tree, Real, Integer, Boolean, Real -> Real
  next-tree-loop (ts) (desired) (i) (found) (best) =
    if i >= list-length ts then (if found then best else desired)
    else next-tree-step ts desired i found best

  next-tree-step : List Tree, Real, Integer, Boolean, Real -> Real
  next-tree-step (ts) (desired) (i) (found) (best) =
    let t = list-at ts i
    in let take = if t.across > 0.0 then (if t.along >= desired then (if found then t.along < best else True) else False) else False
    in if take then next-tree-loop ts desired (i + 1) True t.along
       else next-tree-loop ts desired (i + 1) found best

  next-tree-along : List Tree, Real -> Real
  next-tree-along (ts) (desired) = next-tree-loop ts desired 0 False 0.0

 Each segment's heading vs north, accumulated from the previous segments' exit
 turns. Segment 0 is zero.

  heading-step : Integer -> Real
  heading-step (i) =
    let c = list-at route i
    in (if c.turn-deg >= 0.0 then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0) * (real-abs c.turn-deg * deg)

  heading-at : Integer -> Real
  heading-at (i) = if i <= 0 then 0.0 else heading-at (i - 1) + heading-step (i - 1)

 How many pig legs have appeared at or before this one -- the first
 pig-novelty-count of them are the DISTRACTION herds.

  pig-count-to : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pig-count-to (i) (acc) =
    if i <= 0 then acc
    else pig-count-to (i - 1) (if (list-at route (i - 1)).pigs then acc + 1 else acc)

  Segment = record {
    length : Real,
    width : Real,
    trees : List Tree,
    cows : List Critter,
    pigs : List Critter,
    pigs-distract : Boolean,
    exit-angle : Real,
    exit-right : Boolean,
    exit-to : Integer,
    commit-along : Real,
    north-heading : Real,
    has-mid-tower : Boolean,
    has-cat : Boolean,
    cat : Cat,
    terminates : Boolean,
    exit-creature : Creature
  }

 The rider crosses the next segment's inner-edge extension half-a-width over
 tan-theta before the segment's end and commits to the turn. The terminus has no
 turn, so he commits at the end.

  segment-at : Integer -> Segment
  segment-at (i) =
    let c = list-at route i
    in let angle = real-abs c.turn-deg * deg
    in let trees = fill-trees c.scheme c.length tree-start-inset 0 0
    in let distract = if c.pigs then pig-count-to (i + 1) 0 <= pig-novelty-count else False
    in Segment {
      length = c.length,
      width = lane-width,
      trees = trees,
      cows = fill-cows c.bull,
      pigs = if c.pigs then (if distract then fill-pig-herd c.length else fill-pig-row c.length) else [],
      pigs-distract = distract,
      exit-angle = angle,
      exit-right = c.turn-deg >= 0.0,
      exit-to = if c.terminates then i else i + 1,
      commit-along = if c.terminates then c.length else c.length - (lane-width / 2.0) / r-tan angle,
      north-heading = heading-at i,
      has-mid-tower = c.length > mid-tower-min-length,
      has-cat = c.cat,
      cat = cat-make (lane-width / 2.0) tree-road-offset (next-tree-along trees cat-along),
      terminates = c.terminates,
      exit-creature = if c.terminates then NoCreature else c.creature
    }

 THE CAT IS BUILT FOR EVERY SEGMENT, not only the ones that have one. The zig
 leaves the field `undefined` when has_cat is false, which Codex has no way to
 say and no reason to want; building it costs one record and reading it when
 has-cat is false is the caller's error either way. Nothing observable changes.

  segments-from : Integer -> List Segment
  segments-from (i) =
    if i >= list-length route then []
    else [segment-at i] & segments-from (i + 1)

  build-world : List Segment
  build-world = segments-from 0

Section: Distances

 The sum of the segment lengths, with intersection arcs ignored as negligible --
 the distance the truck's chase schedule lerps its lead down over.

  course-length-from : List Segment, Integer -> Real
  course-length-from (ss) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ss then 0.0
    else (list-at ss i).length + course-length-from ss (i + 1)

  course-length : List Segment -> Real
  course-length (ss) = course-length-from ss 0

 How far along the course a point sits: the lengths of all earlier segments in
 route order, plus the along. The segments ARE in route order, so the prefix is
 just a sum.

  route-distance-from : List Segment, Integer, Integer -> Real
  route-distance-from (ss) (seg) (i) =
    if i >= seg then 0.0
    else (list-at ss i).length + route-distance-from ss seg (i + 1)

  route-distance : List Segment, Integer, Real -> Real
  route-distance (ss) (seg) (along) = route-distance-from ss seg 0 + along

Chapter: Geom
  cites Safari chapter Trig

 Pure rider-relative geometry: the coordinate types, the world-to-rider transform,
 the ground curvature, and near-plane clipping. No projection -- that is Camera --
 no drawing, no allocation. The math leaf the rest of the camera stands on.

 A port of wasm/geom.zig.

 We say:

Section: Coordinates

 A ground-plane point measured FROM THE RIDER: how far forward, down his look
 axis, and how far to his right. The fundamental coordinate of the whole scene.

  RiderPt = record {
    right : Real,
    forward : Real
  }

 A rider-relative vertex carrying a height off the ground -- what the road quads
 and raised structures clip and project as.

  Vec3 = record {
    right : Real,
    forward : Real,
    height : Real
  }

 A point in a segment's bottom-left frame: `a` along, `x` across-from-left.

  AX = record {
    a : Real,
    x : Real
  }

Section: Ground

 The local ground is a gentle spherical plateau: a point drops this far below the
 rider's tangent plane at horizontal distance d, so the road bends toward a finite
 horizon instead of a vanishing point at infinity. Larger radius = gentler.

  ground-radius : Real
  ground-radius = 100000.0

  ground-drop : Real, Real -> Real
  ground-drop (right) (forward) = (right * right + forward * forward) / (2.0 * ground-radius)

Section: The Rider Transform

 to-rider maps a point in a segment's bottom-left frame -- `a` along the segment,
 `x` across from its LEFT edge -- into the rider's frame, given his pose along and
 across the segment's centre line and his look angle `yaw`. His pose is
 centre-relative, so the half-width `hw` shifts x to from-the-left.

  to-rider : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  to-rider (a) (x) (cam-along) (cam-across) (yaw) (hw) =
    let d-a = a - cam-along
    in let d-x = x - (cam-across + hw)
    in let c = r-cos yaw
    in let s = r-sin yaw
    in RiderPt { forward = d-a * c + d-x * s, right = (0.0 - d-a) * s + d-x * c }

Section: The Frame Fusion

 next-to-cur maps a point from the NEXT segment's bottom-left frame into the
 CURRENT segment's, across the join. LEFT and RIGHT are NOT mirror images: a left
 turn fuses on the end-left edge, where the origin is the inner corner; a right
 turn fuses on the end-right edge and pays a width shift. `turns-right` is the
 turn direction.

  next-to-cur : Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real -> AX
  next-to-cur (a-b) (x-b) (seg-len) (theta) (turns-right) (width) =
    let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in if turns-right
      then AX { a = a-b * c - x-b * s + width * s + seg-len, x = x-b * c + a-b * s + width * (1.0 - c) }
      else AX { a = a-b * c + x-b * s + seg-len, x = x-b * c - a-b * s }

 cur-to-next is the inverse: a point in the CURRENT segment's frame expressed in
 the NEXT segment's, across the same join. Used to draw the joint just BEHIND the
 rider, so the pavement he is still crossing does not vanish.

  cur-to-next : Real, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real -> AX
  cur-to-next (a) (x) (seg-len) (theta) (turns-right) (width) =
    let c = r-cos theta
    in let s = r-sin theta
    in if turns-right
      then let a0 = a - seg-len - width * s
        in let x0 = x - width * (1.0 - c)
        in AX { a = a0 * c + x0 * s, x = (0.0 - a0) * s + x0 * c }
      else let a0 = a - seg-len
        in AX { a = a0 * c - x * s, x = a0 * s + x * c }

Section: Line Intersection

 line-meet returns where two infinite lines cross in the rider frame: line A
 through a0 toward a1, line B through b0 toward b1. Used for the corner's outer
 apex, where the two segments' outer shoulders meet. A real turn has a non-zero
 angle, so the lines are never parallel.

  line-meet : RiderPt, RiderPt, RiderPt, RiderPt -> RiderPt
  line-meet (a0) (a1) (b0) (b1) =
    let dax = a1.right - a0.right
    in let daf = a1.forward - a0.forward
    in let dbx = b1.right - b0.right
    in let dbf = b1.forward - b0.forward
    in let t = ((b0.right - a0.right) * dbf - (b0.forward - a0.forward) * dbx) / (dax * dbf - daf * dbx)
    in RiderPt { right = a0.right + t * dax, forward = a0.forward + t * daf }

Section: Near-Plane Clipping

 Clip a rider-frame polygon against the near plane at `near` metres forward, so no
 vertex sits behind the eye where the perspective divide would fling it across the
 screen. Fewer than 3 vertices out means the caller skips the polygon.

 The zig writes into a caller-supplied `out` slice and returns a count; this
 returns the list, which is the same thing without the buffer. The wrap-around
 edge is the reason the index walks to the length and reads (i + 1) modulo it: the
 polygon is closed, so the last vertex pairs with the first.

  clip-cross : Vec3, Vec3, Real -> List Vec3
  clip-cross (a) (b) (near) =
    let f = (near - a.forward) / (b.forward - a.forward)
    in [Vec3 { right = a.right + f * (b.right - a.right), forward = near, height = a.height + f * (b.height - a.height) }]

  both-sides : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  both-sides (a-in) (b-in) = if a-in then b-in else (if b-in then False else True)

  clip-near-edge : List Vec3, Real, Integer -> List Vec3
  clip-near-edge (poly) (near) (i) =
    let n = list-length poly
    in if i >= n then []
    else let a = list-at poly i
    in let b = list-at poly ((i + 1) - (((i + 1) / n) * n))
    in let a-in = a.forward >= near
    in let b-in = b.forward >= near
    in let kept = if a-in then [a] else []
    in let crossed = if both-sides a-in b-in then [] else clip-cross a b near
    in kept & crossed & clip-near-edge poly near (i + 1)

  clip-near : List Vec3, Real -> List Vec3
  clip-near (poly) (near) = clip-near-edge poly near 0

Chapter: Camera
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom

 The perspective projection: a rider-relative ground point at a height becomes a
 screen pixel. Owns the lens constants and the near plane. Pure; no drawing, no
 allocation.

 A port of wasm/camera.zig.

 ONE ADAPTATION. The zig carries the live projection width as a mutable global,
 `pub var view_w`, which the native window widens toward the screen's aspect while
 FOCAL stays frozen -- that is what makes a wider window a wider field of view
 rather than a zoom. Codex has no mutable global, so view-w is threaded as a
 parameter. It is the camera's only mutable state, and threading it is the pure
 equivalent rather than a change of behaviour: pass camera-w for the design width
 the browser and the golden-PNG harness both use.

 We say:

Section: Lens

  camera-w : Real
  camera-w = 960.0

  camera-h : Real
  camera-h = 600.0

  eye-h : Real
  eye-h = 1.2

  near : Real
  near = 0.4

  fov-deg : Real
  fov-deg = 70.0

 The base focal, in pixels, looking straight ahead. FROZEN at the design width and
 NOT recomputed from the live view-w -- that is what keeps a wider window a wider
 FOV. The live cam-focal pulls IN from this as the rider leans or narrows focus.

  focal : Real
  focal = (camera-w / 2.0) / r-tan (fov-deg / 2.0 * deg)

Section: Focal Pull-In

 The focal pulls IN -- widening the FOV, a smaller focal -- for the deeper of two
 narrowings: the LEAN into a turn, and the rider's ATTENTION on something off the
 road. The lean narrows hardest and is squared so it snaps in deep in the turn;
 attention narrows gentler and linearly, but a crossing cat's attention overshoots
 1, so it can pull harder than a full gaze. They rarely overlap -- eyes on the road
 mid-corner -- so the tightest simply wins.

  min-focal-factor : Real
  min-focal-factor = 0.35

  min-gaze-focal-factor : Real
  min-gaze-focal-factor = 0.61

  focal-for-lean : Real -> Real
  focal-for-lean (lean-frac) = focal * (1.0 - (1.0 - min-focal-factor) * lean-frac * lean-frac)

  focal-for-gaze : Real -> Real
  focal-for-gaze (attention) = focal * (1.0 - (1.0 - min-gaze-focal-factor) * attention)

  cam-focal : Real, Real -> Real
  cam-focal (lean-frac) (attention) =
    let a = focal-for-lean lean-frac
    in let b = focal-for-gaze attention
    in if a < b then a else b

Section: Projection

  ScreenPt = record {
    x : Real,
    y : Real
  }

 The perspective divide is by forward, so callers must clip to the near plane
 first -- forward is then never near zero.

  project : Vec3, Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  project (p) (cf) (view-w) =
    ScreenPt {
      x = view-w / 2.0 + (p.right / p.forward) * cf,
      y = camera-h / 2.0 - ((p.height - eye-h) / p.forward) * cf
    }

Chapter: Paint
  cites Safari chapter Camera

 The seam to JS: a draw command in wasm/paint.zig's wire.

 The zig writes each command into a fixed pre-sized buffer in linear memory and
 hands the browser a byte length. The port is pure, so it RETURNS commands and the
 wasm shim does the writing -- the same split NOTES calls seam 3, and the reason
 one grader serves every module: a frame is a flat list either way.

 The wire:

   tag 0   [0][color][nPoints][x f32][y f32]...
   tag 1   [1][color][strength f32][nPoints][x f32][y f32]...
   tag 3   [3][color][x f32][y f32][r f32][alpha f32]
   tag 4   [4][rgbaCentre][rgbaEdge][cx][cy][r][nPoints][x][y]...
   tag 5   [5][rgba0][rgba1][off0][off1][ax][ay][bx][by][nPoints][x][y]...
   tag 6   [6][rgba0][rgba1][off0][off1][cx][cy][ux][uy][vx][vy][nPoints][x][y]...

 tag 0 is a solid fill; tag 1 is a horizontal round gradient -- dark edge, bright
 centre, dark edge across the polygon's x-extent -- with a per-polygon strength
 that lets crown shading fade to flat with distance instead of popping. strength
 rides on every command so the record has one shape; tag 0 ignores it, exactly as
 the blitter does.

 Coordinates are SCREEN pixels here and still Real. They narrow to f32 in the
 shim, at the buffer, which is the only place the game's width is load-bearing.

 We say:

Section: Commands

  DrawCmd = record {
    tag : Integer,
    color : Integer,
    color2 : Integer,
    strength : Real,
    geom : List Real,
    pts : List Real
  }

 TWO FIELDS THE GRADIENT TAGS FORCED, and taking them changed the record's rule
 for the better. `pts` is now ALWAYS the polygon and `geom` is always the
 command's own parameters -- a beacon's centre and radius, a gradient's stops and
 axes. Before, the beacon smuggled its x, y and r through `pts`, which meant `pts`
 sometimes held points and sometimes did not, and every reader had to know which.
 Now one rule covers every tag.

 `color2` is the second stop. Tags 0, 1 and 3 leave it zero and leave `geom` empty
 or short; nothing reads a field its tag does not define.

  flatten-screen : List ScreenPt, Integer -> List Real
  flatten-screen (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else let p = list-at ps i
    in [p.x, p.y] & flatten-screen ps (i + 1)

  push-poly : Integer, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-poly (color) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 0, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = 0.0, geom = [], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

 pushRoundPoly drops a polygon with fewer than three points rather than emitting
 a degenerate one; push-poly is given the same guard so the two agree.

  push-round-poly : Integer, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-round-poly (color) (strength) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 1, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = strength, geom = [], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

 THE BEACON IS NOT A POLYGON, and it is the first command here that is not. It is
 a disc -- a centre, a radius and an alpha -- and the blitter draws a true arc for
 it. It gets its own tag because unlike every other command it is NOT OPAQUE, and
 the colour word on the solid path carries no alpha to say so.

 It still fits the one DrawCmd shape, which is the point of that shape. The
 record's prose already argues that strength rides on every command so there is
 one record rather than two; the same argument carries the disc. `strength` is the
 alpha the blitter composites at, and `pts` holds x, y and r -- three Reals where a
 polygon holds an even number of them. The shim branches on the tag when it
 writes, exactly as paint.zig's own pushBeacon does.

  push-beacon : Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  push-beacon (color) (x) (y) (r) (alpha) =
    [DrawCmd { tag = 3, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = alpha, geom = [x, y, r], pts = [] }]

Section: The Gradient Fills

 Three more tags, all polygons with a gradient instead of a flat colour, and all
 three carry TWO colours in 0xAARRGGBB rather than the 0xRRGGBB the solid path
 uses -- because these composite, and the alpha is what makes a headlight beam a
 beam rather than a white wedge.

 Tag 4 is a RADIAL fill from a centre out to a radius: the truck's headlight beams
 and their glow. Tags 5 and 6 are 2-stop gradients with explicit stop offsets --
 5 linear along an axis, 6 radial through an ELLIPSE given by two axis vectors --
 and they are the bull's shading panels, the one animal whose art is the Fluent
 Color variant rather than the Flat one.

 All three drop a polygon with fewer than three points, exactly as the solid path
 does and for the same reason.

  push-grad-poly : Integer, Integer, Real, Real, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-grad-poly (rgba-center) (rgba-edge) (cx) (cy) (r) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 4, color = rgba-center, color2 = rgba-edge, strength = 0.0, geom = [cx, cy, r], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

  push-linear-grad-poly : Integer, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-linear-grad-poly (rgba0) (rgba1) (off0) (off1) (ax) (ay) (bx) (by) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 5, color = rgba0, color2 = rgba1, strength = 0.0, geom = [off0, off1, ax, ay, bx, by], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

  push-radial-grad-poly : Integer, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, List ScreenPt -> List DrawCmd
  push-radial-grad-poly (rgba0) (rgba1) (off0) (off1) (cx) (cy) (ux) (uy) (vx) (vy) (ps) =
    if list-length ps < 3 then []
    else [DrawCmd { tag = 6, color = rgba0, color2 = rgba1, strength = 0.0, geom = [off0, off1, cx, cy, ux, uy, vx, vy], pts = flatten-screen ps 0 }]

Chapter: Pond

 pond -- a small pond at a corner (seg13), just BEYOND a right-turn intersection,
 off to its LEFT, with ducks floating on the water. Pure data and dimensions; the
 mapping, projection and emit live in the renderer, the same split safari-critter
 uses.

 Frame: cv = metres BEYOND the incoming segment's end edge (the rider's forward as
 he arrives), cu = metres across from its end-left corner (0 = left edge, + = toward
 the road; the pond's cu is NEGATIVE, out to the left -- the outer side of the right
 turn).

 A port of wasm/pond.zig. The zig carries these as f32 and this chapter as Real
 (f64), so the judge compares with a tolerance rather than for equality; every
 value here is integral except duck-height, so the tolerance buys nothing yet and
 the comparison is effectively exact.

 We say:

Section: Shapes

  PondPt = record {
    cu : Real,
    cv : Real
  }

  Duck = record {
    p : PondPt,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

Section: Water

 The outline is a big blob off the LEFT of the road, ~30m across (cu -1 to -31)
 reaching ~30m beyond the intersection (cv 3 to 32). Its near edge (the flat front
 at cv 3) faces the incoming road.

  water-outline : List PondPt
  water-outline =
    [ PondPt { cu = -2.0, cv = 3.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -28.0, cv = 3.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -31.0, cv = 14.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 28.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 32.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -1.0, cv = 16.0 } ]

  water-color : Integer
  water-color = 3112588

Section: Bank

 A 1m khaki bank along the water's FAR edge -- the shore. Its inner edge hugs the
 water's far edge (the arc cv 29 to 32 to 28), its outer edge is 1m further onto
 the land.

  bank : List PondPt
  bank =
    [ PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 32.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 28.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -26.0, cv = 29.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -15.0, cv = 33.0 },
      PondPt { cu = -5.0, cv = 30.0 } ]

  bank-color : Integer
  bank-color = 12759680

Section: Ducks

 Six ducks floating on the water, spread across the pond -- three deeper in (larger
 cv) and three nearer the intersection (smaller cv, toward the front edge at cv 3).
 ADULTS, drawn slightly out of proportion so they read. cu is shifted toward the
 road (less negative) than the pond centre, so the ducks hug the near/road side of
 the water while still sitting inside the outline.

  duck-codepoint : Integer
  duck-codepoint = 129414

  duck-height : Real
  duck-height = 0.9

  ducks : List Duck
  ducks =
    [ Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -8.0, cv = 11.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -16.0, cv = 17.0 }, face-right = False },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -9.0, cv = 21.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -4.0, cv = 6.0 }, face-right = True },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -12.0, cv = 7.0 }, face-right = False },
      Duck { p = PondPt { cu = -20.0, cv = 8.0 }, face-right = True } ]

Chapter: Tower
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The radio tower beyond each intersection: a tall square-base lattice pyramid of
 metal rods tapering to an apex with a pink beacon. The owner places it; this
 chapter only renders.

 A port of wasm/tower.zig.

 THIS IS THE FLAT FORM ONLY -- the four faces drawn as a billboard lattice, which
 is what the TypeScript draws beyond its far distance. The zig diverges from the
 TypeScript in ONE way and the port keeps the divergence: every rod stays a 3D
 segment and is near-clipped BEFORE projecting, rather than interpolating points
 that are already on screen. The TypeScript could skip that because it only used
 the flat path for far towers, where nothing straddles the eye plane; here the
 flat path runs at all distances, so a tower you pass close would otherwise streak
 a rod clear across the viewport.

 The apex beacon BLINKS on the step clock, as a pure function of it, so it freezes
 on pause and runs backwards on reverse -- which is why the phase can be negative
 and why the modulo has to be the floored one.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

  tower-height : Real
  tower-height = 80.0

 Half the twelve-metre square base edge.

  tower-half : Real
  tower-half = 6.0

 A cross-beam ring every this many metres, and X-braces on the bottom two stages.

  stage-height : Real
  stage-height = 20.0

  brace-stages : Integer
  brace-stages = 2

  rod-half : Real
  rod-half = 0.12

  rod-w : Real
  rod-w = rod-half * 2.0

  tower-metal : Integer
  tower-metal = 10133672

 A local ground bulge, stronger than the road's, so a far tower sinks until its
 base meets the horizon.

  earth-radius : Real
  earth-radius = 20000.0

  beacon-radius : Real
  beacon-radius = 3.0

  beacon-color : Integer
  beacon-color = 16723942

 Frames for a full invisible-to-bright-to-invisible blink.

  beacon-period : Real
  beacon-period = 120.0

Section: The Beacon Clock

 Spread each tower's blink phase so they do not pulse in unison: 37 is coprime to
 the 120-frame period, so successive owners land on distinct offsets.

  beacon-offset-for : Integer -> Real
  beacon-offset-for (n) = real-from-int ((n * 37) - (((n * 37) / 120) * 120))

 Brightness is 0 at the cycle ends and 1 in the middle.

 THE DOUBLE MODULO IS THE ZIG'S AND IS KEPT. `@mod` is already non-negative for a
 positive divisor, so `@mod(@mod(p, P) + P, P)` is belt and braces -- but the port
 is a port, and a phase really can be negative here because the clock runs
 backwards on reverse.

  beacon-brightness : Real -> Real
  beacon-brightness (phase) =
    let wrapped = mod-real (mod-real phase beacon-period + beacon-period) beacon-period
    in (1.0 - r-cos (2.0 * pi * wrapped / beacon-period)) / 2.0

Section: The Square

 The four base corners in half-base units, before the yaw.

  corner-u : Integer -> Real
  corner-u (k) = if k == 0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 1 then 1.0 else if k == 2 then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0

  corner-v : Integer -> Real
  corner-v (k) = if k == 0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 1 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 2 then 1.0 else 1.0

 Base corner k in the owning segment's bottom-left frame, given the tower's base
 centre and yaw. The caller maps it into the rider frame. At the apex the square
 shrinks to a point at the centre, so the apex needs no helper.

  base-corner-ax : Integer, Real, Real, Real -> AX
  base-corner-ax (k) (a0) (x0) (yaw) =
    let du = corner-u k * tower-half
    in let dv = corner-v k * tower-half
    in let cy = r-cos yaw
    in let sy = r-sin yaw
    in AX { a = a0 + (du * sy + dv * cy), x = x0 + (du * cy - dv * sy) }

Section: Geometry

  tower-ground-drop : RiderPt -> Real
  tower-ground-drop (p) = (p.right * p.right + p.forward * p.forward) / (2.0 * earth-radius)

  lerp3v : Vec3, Vec3, Real -> Vec3
  lerp3v (a) (b) (t) =
    Vec3 {
      right = a.right + (b.right - a.right) * t,
      forward = a.forward + (b.forward - a.forward) * t,
      height = a.height + (b.height - a.height) * t
    }

 Cross-section corner k at world height h, lowered by the ground drop. The square
 shrinks linearly toward the apex at the centre, so it is a base-to-centre lerp by
 h over the height. The corner stays a 3D point so each rod can be near-clipped
 before projecting.

  corner-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Integer, Real, Real -> Vec3
  corner-at (base) (center) (k) (h) (drop) =
    let t = h / tower-height
    in let bk = list-at base k
    in Vec3 { right = bk.right + (center.right - bk.right) * t, forward = bk.forward + (center.forward - bk.forward) * t, height = h - drop }

Section: Rods

 A thick screen-space line as a filled quad of width wpx.

  bar : ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar (a) (b) (wpx) =
    let dx = b.x - a.x
    in let dy = b.y - a.y
    in let raw = real-sqrt (dx * dx + dy * dy)
    in let len = if raw < 0.0001 then 1.0 else raw
    in rod-quad a b ((0.0 - dy) / len * wpx / 2.0) (dx / len * wpx / 2.0)

 NAMED FOR ITS SECTION RATHER THAN ITS SHAPE, and the rename was forced by the
 bare-metal arm: `bar-quad` is also GuardRail's, and Codex chapters share one flat
 namespace. Each chapter sees its own definition so both compiled and ran
 correctly -- but a mention in a chapter that defines NEITHER resolves by the
 order the build globs files, which is not a property anything should rely on.
 The seed diagnoses it (CDX3006); the zig arm says nothing.

  rod-quad : ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rod-quad (a) (b) (ox) (oy) =
    push-poly tower-metal [ScreenPt { x = a.x + ox, y = a.y + oy }, ScreenPt { x = b.x + ox, y = b.y + oy }, ScreenPt { x = b.x - ox, y = b.y - oy }, ScreenPt { x = a.x - ox, y = a.y - oy }]

 One rod, near-clipped: a 3D segment clipped to the near plane before projecting,
 so a rod straddling the eye plane cannot fling a quad across the viewport. Both
 ends behind means skip; one behind means pull it to the plane.

  bar3d : Vec3, Vec3, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar3d (a) (b) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    let a-in = a.forward >= near
    in let b-in = b.forward >= near
    in if a-in then (if b-in then bar3d-draw a b wpx cf view-w else bar3d-draw a (bar3d-cut a b) wpx cf view-w)
       else (if b-in then bar3d-draw (bar3d-cut a b) b wpx cf view-w else [])

  bar3d-cut : Vec3, Vec3 -> Vec3
  bar3d-cut (a) (b) = lerp3v a b ((near - a.forward) / (b.forward - a.forward))

  bar3d-draw : Vec3, Vec3, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar3d-draw (a) (b) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    bar (project a cf view-w) (project b cf view-w) wpx

Section: The Tower

 Rod width in pixels at this depth, from two ground points one metre apart.

  rod-px : Real, Real, Real -> Real
  rod-px (forward) (cf) (view-w) =
    let p1 = project (Vec3 { right = 1.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let p0 = project (Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in rod-w * (p1.x - p0.x)


  tower-legs : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Vec3, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tower-legs (base) (center) (apex) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else (bar3d (corner-at base center k clip-h drop) apex wpx cf view-w) & tower-legs base center apex clip-h drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

 Cross-beam rings at 20, 40 and 60, above the drop.

  ring-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  ring-at (base) (center) (h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else (bar3d (corner-at base center k h drop) (corner-at base center ((k + 1) - (((k + 1) / 4) * 4)) h drop) wpx cf view-w) & ring-at base center h drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

  rings : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rings (base) (center) (h) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    if h >= tower-height then []
    else (if h <= clip-h then [] else ring-at base center h drop wpx cf view-w 0) & rings base center (h + stage-height) clip-h drop wpx cf view-w

 X-braces on the bottom stages, each diagonal clipped at the drop height.

  brace-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-at (base) (center) (lo) (hi) (f) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else brace-pair base center lo hi f drop wpx cf view-w k

  brace-pair : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-pair (base) (center) (lo) (hi) (f) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    let j = (k + 1) - (((k + 1) / 4) * 4)
    in let kj = bar3d (lerp3v (corner-at base center k lo drop) (corner-at base center j hi drop) f) (corner-at base center j hi drop) wpx cf view-w
    in let jk = bar3d (lerp3v (corner-at base center j lo drop) (corner-at base center k hi drop) f) (corner-at base center k hi drop) wpx cf view-w
    in kj & jk & brace-at base center lo hi f drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

  braces : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  braces (base) (center) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (stage) =
    if stage >= brace-stages then []
    else brace-stage base center clip-h drop wpx cf view-w stage

  brace-stage : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-stage (base) (center) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (stage) =
    let lo = real-from-int stage * stage-height
    in let hi = lo + stage-height
    in let rest = braces base center clip-h drop wpx cf view-w (stage + 1)
    in if hi <= clip-h then rest
       else (brace-at base center lo hi ((real-max lo clip-h - lo) / stage-height) drop wpx cf view-w 0) & rest

 The apex beacon: a pink disc whose SIZE scales with distance but whose BRIGHTNESS
 is the blink alpha -- a depthless glow composited over whatever is behind. Skips
 the invisible part of the cycle, and a sub-pixel disc.

  draw-beacon : ScreenPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-beacon (apex-s) (forward) (cf) (view-w) (bright) =
    if bright < 0.02 then []
    else beacon-disc apex-s forward cf view-w bright

  beacon-disc : ScreenPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  beacon-disc (apex-s) (forward) (cf) (view-w) (bright) =
    let p1 = project (Vec3 { right = 1.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let p0 = project (Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let r = beacon-radius * (p1.x - p0.x)
    in if r < 0.5 then [] else push-beacon beacon-color apex-s.x apex-s.y r bright

 Render a tower given its four base corners and base centre already mapped into
 the rider frame. Sinks it by the local ground drop; a tower sunk past its own
 height is below the horizon and is not drawn at all.

  draw-flat : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) =
    if center.forward < near then []
    else draw-flat-body base center cf view-w beacon-phase (tower-ground-drop center)

  draw-flat-body : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat-body (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) (drop) =
    if drop >= tower-height then []
    else draw-flat-rods base center cf view-w beacon-phase drop

  draw-flat-rods : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat-rods (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) (drop) =
    let apex = Vec3 { right = center.right, forward = center.forward, height = tower-height - drop }
    in let wpx = rod-px center.forward cf view-w
    in let l = tower-legs base center apex drop drop wpx cf view-w 0
    in let r = rings base center stage-height drop drop wpx cf view-w
    in let x = braces base center drop drop wpx cf view-w 0
    in let b = draw-beacon (project apex cf view-w) center.forward cf view-w (beacon-brightness beacon-phase)
    in l & r & x & b

Chapter: GuardRail
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The metal barrier on the outer edge of a corner: one horizontal bar on thin
 upright posts. Render builds the PATH the rail follows along the ground -- run-up
 along the outer shoulder, the two legs into and out of the apex, run-out -- and
 this chapter raises that path into 3D rail polygons.

 A port of wasm/guard_rail.zig.

 WHY IT COLLECTS INSTEAD OF DRAWING. Rails, trees and critters share ONE
 back-to-front depth pass, so a tree standing behind a rail is occluded by it.
 Each rail poly therefore carries its own mean forward depth and is merged into
 render's sort; the clip-project-push happens later, in sorted order. Drawing
 rails in a separate pass under all scenery is the exact bug that once made a far
 tree paint over a near rail. So rail-emit and rail-draw-poly are deliberately two
 functions, and the ORDERING BETWEEN THEM BELONGS TO RENDER.

 The bar is a quad STRIP -- one quad per path segment -- not the single long
 ribbon the TypeScript draws. Perceptually identical, since consecutive quads
 share an exact edge, but every poly stays at four vertices, so it near-clips
 cleanly and sorts by an honest per-poly depth. Heights are raw, with no ground
 curvature, exactly as in the TypeScript: the rail is always close and the drop is
 sub-millimetre.

 THIS IS THE FIRST CHAPTER TO CITE `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` RATHER THAN THE
 PORT'S OWN STAND-IN. real-sqrt does not call dm-reduce, so it never reaches the
 real-from-int the zig plug cannot emit, and the emitter emits only what the entry
 reaches. `Safari chapter Trig` is still needed for sine and cosine and is still a
 stand-in; the square root is not one. Nothing here should acquire a Trig name.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 The bar's centre above the ground, its vertical thickness, and each upright
 post's width -- all in metres.

  rail-height : Real
  rail-height = 0.5

  rail-thickness : Real
  rail-thickness = 0.1

  rail-post-width : Real
  rail-post-width = 0.02

 The bar reads bright metallic; the posts sit a touch darker behind it.

  rail-metal : Integer
  rail-metal = 12765135

  rail-post-metal : Integer
  rail-post-metal = 10133672

 How far the rail runs past the corner along each outer edge. Render's to use, but
 it is the rail's dimension and the zig publishes it here.

  rail-runout : Integer
  rail-runout = 10

 The zig's store is a fixed array whose add silently returns once full. The cap is
 sized so that cannot fire on this route, but a silent drop is still the stated
 behaviour, so the port keeps it -- as a list-take over the finished list, which is
 exactly the same thing: bars are added before posts in both, so truncating the
 concatenation drops precisely the elements the zig would never have stored.

  max-rail-polys : Integer
  max-rail-polys = 3072

  bar-top : Real
  bar-top = rail-height + rail-thickness / 2.0

  bar-bot : Real
  bar-bot = rail-height - rail-thickness / 2.0

  half-post : Real
  half-post = rail-post-width / 2.0

Section: A Raised Poly

 One raised rail poly in the rider frame -- always four vertices -- carrying its
 fill colour and its mean forward depth, the key render sorts it by.

  RailPoly = record {
    v : List Vec3,
    color : Integer,
    fwd : Real
  }

  rail-poly : Vec3, Vec3, Vec3, Vec3, Integer -> RailPoly
  rail-poly (p0) (p1) (p2) (p3) (color) =
    let fwd = (p0.forward + p1.forward + p2.forward + p3.forward) / 4.0
    in RailPoly { v = [p0, p1, p2, p3], color = color, fwd = fwd }

Section: The Bar

 A quad strip between consecutive path points, bottom edge at bar-bot and top edge
 at bar-top. Consecutive quads share an edge exactly, so it reads as one band.

  bar-quad : RiderPt, RiderPt -> RailPoly
  bar-quad (p) (q) =
    let p-bot = Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = bar-bot }
    in let q-bot = Vec3 { right = q.right, forward = q.forward, height = bar-bot }
    in let q-top = Vec3 { right = q.right, forward = q.forward, height = bar-top }
    in let p-top = Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = bar-top }
    in rail-poly p-bot q-bot q-top p-top rail-metal

  bars : List RiderPt, Integer -> List RailPoly
  bars (path) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length path then []
    else [bar-quad (list-at path i) (list-at path (i + 1))] & bars path (i + 1)

Section: The Posts

 A slim upright at each path point, its width laid ALONG the local run direction so
 it reads edge-on from the road. The direction is taken from the neighbours on
 either side, clamped at both ends of the path.

 THE ZERO TEST IS ~0, BITWISE EXACT, NOT ~. The zig writes `if (len == 0)`, which
 is a bitwise comparison, and Codex refuses a bare == between Reals (CDX2085), so
 the caller has to say which equality it meant. A 4-ULP ~ would call a
 nearly-coincident pair of path points coincident and swing that post to the fixed
 fallback direction instead of its own -- a visible wrong answer, not a rounding
 one. Same call as the hull sort in Tree, for the same reason.

  post-box : RiderPt, Real, Real -> RailPoly
  post-box (p) (ox) (ofwd) =
    let back-foot = Vec3 { right = p.right - ox, forward = p.forward - ofwd, height = 0.0 }
    in let fore-foot = Vec3 { right = p.right + ox, forward = p.forward + ofwd, height = 0.0 }
    in let fore-head = Vec3 { right = p.right + ox, forward = p.forward + ofwd, height = bar-top }
    in let back-head = Vec3 { right = p.right - ox, forward = p.forward - ofwd, height = bar-top }
    in rail-poly back-foot fore-foot fore-head back-head rail-post-metal

  post-quad : List RiderPt, Integer -> RailPoly
  post-quad (path) (i) =
    let n = list-length path
    in let ia = if i == 0 then 0 else i - 1
    in let ib = if i + 1 >= n then n - 1 else i + 1
    in let a = list-at path ia
    in let b = list-at path ib
    in let dr = b.right - a.right
    in let df = b.forward - a.forward
    in let raw = real-sqrt (dr * dr + df * df)
    in let run = if raw ~0 0.0 then 1.0 else raw
    in let ox = dr / run * half-post
    in let ofwd = df / run * half-post
    in post-box (list-at path i) ox ofwd

  posts : List RiderPt, Integer -> List RailPoly
  posts (path) (i) =
    if i >= list-length path then []
    else [post-quad path i] & posts path (i + 1)

Section: Emit

 Raise the ground path -- its centreline in the rider frame, one post per point --
 into the bar and its posts. A path of fewer than two points has no run direction
 and raises nothing.

  rail-emit : List RiderPt -> List RailPoly
  rail-emit (path) =
    if list-length path < 2 then []
    else list-take ((bars path 0) & posts path 0) max-rail-polys

Section: Draw

 Near-clip one stored rail poly, project it, and push the fill -- the same pipeline
 as a road quad, but the corners carry their own heights so it stands above the
 pavement. Called by render in depth-sorted order.

  project-all : List Vec3, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  project-all (ps) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [project (list-at ps i) cf view-w] & project-all ps cf view-w (i + 1)

  rail-draw-poly : RailPoly, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rail-draw-poly (rp) (cf) (view-w) =
    let clipped = clip-near rp.v near
    in if list-length clipped < 3 then []
    else push-poly rp.color (project-all clipped cf view-w 0)

 A convenience for a caller that already holds the polys in the order it wants them
 painted. THE ORDER IS THE CALLER'S: render merges rails into one depth sort with
 the trees and the critters, and this walks the list exactly as given.

  rail-draw-all : List RailPoly, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  rail-draw-all (ps) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else (rail-draw-poly (list-at ps i) cf view-w) & rail-draw-all ps cf view-w (i + 1)

Chapter: SafariCritter
  cites Safari chapter World

 The two big animals parked AT a corner: an ADULT standing just past the far
 (outer) edge as if it had crossed, facing into the turn, and its BABY ahead in
 the rider's path beyond the turn.

 A COMPLETE port of wasm/safari_critter.zig, and it is worth saying why a whole
 file ports here when its neighbours arrive in halves. This one is placement and
 nothing else -- three constants, a species table, and one arithmetic rule the two
 animals share. WHAT DRAWS the pair is critter.zig, over emoji_frames.zig's 182 KB
 of baked polygons, and that is a different file, a different chapter, and the
 same deferral cat.zig already carries.

 THE POND IS THE EXCEPTION, and it is why this returns a LIST rather than a pair.
 A duck pond is a hand-drawn scene -- Safari chapter Pond, water and bank and
 ducks -- not an emoji pair, so it contributes no critters at all and render draws
 it separately. The terminus has no exit corner and contributes none either. The
 zig says that with an optional Species and a returned count; Codex has no null,
 so the count is the list's own length, and it is two or zero and never one.

 The late-route giant upsizing of safari_critter.ts is absent from the zig --
 Steve: never effective -- so it is absent here. The adult/baby sizing is kept.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 Codex has no null, so the species table answers for every creature and `present`
 says whether the answer means anything. The same shape Gaze uses for the pig it
 may or may not have noticed.

  Species = record {
    present : Boolean,
    cp : Integer,
    adult-h : Real
  }

  no-species : Species
  no-species = Species { present = False, cp = 0, adult-h = 0.0 }

 The adult stands this much further off than half its own width, which is what
 keeps it clear of the guard rail rather than straddling it.

  adult-rail-buffer : Real
  adult-rail-buffer = 1.5

 The baby's height as a fraction of the adult, and how far past the turn it stands
 in the rider's path.

  baby-ratio : Real
  baby-ratio = 0.5

  baby-beyond : Real
  baby-beyond = 14.0

 The emoji and the adult height in metres, one row per species. The giraffe stands
 tallest. The rhino is about thirty percent smaller than the elephant on purpose:
 less of it blends into the sky, and it reads against the mountains instead.

  species-of : Creature -> Species
  species-of (c) =
   when c
    is Elephant -> Species { present = True, cp = 128024, adult-h = 2.8 }
    is Giraffe -> Species { present = True, cp = 129426, adult-h = 4.5 }
    is Zebra -> Species { present = True, cp = 129427, adult-h = 1.6 }
    is Rhino -> Species { present = True, cp = 129423, adult-h = 2.2 }
    is DuckPond -> no-species
    is NoCreature -> no-species

Section: The Corner Pair

 Both animals in the FROM segment's frame: `along` measured from that segment's
 start, `across` centre-relative, exactly as the trees and cows are authored.

 THE ADULT SITS ON THE SIDE AWAY FROM THE TURN -- the turn sign is negated -- so a
 right turn parks it left and a left turn parks it right. That is the OUTER corner,
 the one the rider swings widest around and looks straight at through the bend.
 Both animals face INTO the turn, so their eyes lead him around it.

  corner-critters : Creature, Real, Boolean, Real -> List Critter
  corner-critters (c) (along) (turn-right) (hw) =
    let sp = species-of c
    in let turn-sign = if turn-right then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0
    in let adult-h = sp.adult-h
    in if sp.present
      then [Critter { along = along, across = (0.0 - turn-sign) * (hw + adult-rail-buffer + adult-h / 2.0), codepoint = sp.cp, height = adult-h, face-right = turn-right },
            Critter { along = along + baby-beyond, across = 0.0, codepoint = sp.cp, height = adult-h * baby-ratio, face-right = turn-right }]
      else []

Chapter: Render
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Pond
  cites Safari chapter Tower
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Safari chapter SafariCritter

 The frame mapping: which segments are in view, and how a point authored in any of
 their frames reaches the rider's.

 A partial port of wasm/render.zig -- the spine, not yet the frame. render.zig is
 the orchestrator: one 431-line `frame` that walks the chain, lays the road strip
 and each corner's pavement, collects the trees, towers, critters, cats, rails and
 the truck, depth-sorts the lot and draws it back to front. Every one of those
 steps stands on `at (d, a, x)` -- the composition ported here -- so this is the
 piece the rest of the module has to be built on.

 WHAT IS PORTED HERE IS SHAPED BY WHAT RENDER.ZIG LETS ANYTHING SEE, and that is a
 finding rather than a preference. Its entire public surface is four names --
 Chain, Pose, at, and frame. buildChain, Mapper, mapPt, and all four ground and
 rail emitters are private to the file. So `at` is the ONE piece of this chapter
 with a direct oracle: a probe can call it and compare. The chain and the prev
 mapper are graded against what the world and geom already expose, and RenderCheck
 says exactly where that is weaker and why. PORTING_NOTES E1 records it as the
 module's shape, not a gap in the check.

 We say:

Section: The Chain

 How many segments ahead are drawn, and the fixed buffer that bounds them. The zig
 carries both because its Chain is a fixed array that the look-ahead has to fit
 inside; the port keeps both because the SMALLER of the two is what actually stops
 the walk, and which one that is should stay visible rather than being folded into
 one number.

  look-ahead : Integer
  look-ahead = 7

  max-chain : Integer
  max-chain = 8

 THE CHAIN IS A LIST OF SEGMENT INDICES. The zig's Chain is an array plus a length
 because it has to be; a Codex list carries its own length, so the record collapses
 to the list and every `ch.len` becomes `list-length ch`.

 Follow exit-to from `start`, stopping at the cap or at the terminus -- no segment
 leads out of the finish line, so the chain ends there rather than looping. `n` is
 how many indices are already down, which is the zig's `ch.len` read at the TOP of
 its loop: the cap is tested before the index is appended, so a chain is never
 longer than the cap and the terminus is never dropped for being one past it.

  build-chain : List Segment, Integer -> List Integer
  build-chain (segs) (start) = chain-from segs start 0

  chain-from : List Segment, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  chain-from (segs) (s) (n) =
    if n >= look-ahead then []
    else if n >= max-chain then []
    else if (list-at segs s).terminates then [s]
    else [s] & chain-from segs (list-at segs s).exit-to (n + 1)

Section: The Rider Pose

 Where the rider sits on his CURRENT segment and which way he is looking. `hw` is
 that segment's half width, and it is here rather than being looked up because his
 `across` is centre-relative while to-rider wants from-the-left -- the same shift
 Camera's port already threads.

 The `yaw` handed in is not his heading alone. render.zig folds the distracted
 gaze and the lean's head turn into it before building the pose, so the whole view
 swings together with the backdrop; the port takes the sum, as the zig does.

  Pose = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    hw : Real
  }

Section: The Frame Composition

 at maps (a, x) in chain[d]'s bottom-left frame into the rider's, composing the
 joins down to chain[0] and then applying the rider transform. For d = 0 there is
 nothing to compose and it is just to-rider.

 THE JOINS COMPOSE BACKWARDS, from d down to 1, and the segment that supplies each
 join is the one BELOW it. chain[k] leads to chain[k+1] through chain[k]'s OWN exit
 turn, so stepping a point from chain[k+1]'s frame down into chain[k]'s reads
 chain[k]'s length, angle, direction and width -- never chain[k+1]'s. Off by one
 here and a distant segment lands rotated by its neighbour's turn, which does not
 look like corruption; it looks like a plausible road bending the wrong way.

  compose-down : List Segment, List Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> AX
  compose-down (segs) (ch) (k) (a) (x) =
    if k <= 0 then AX { a = a, x = x }
    else let seg = list-at segs (list-at ch (k - 1))
    in let p = next-to-cur a x seg.length seg.exit-angle seg.exit-right seg.width
    in compose-down segs ch (k - 1) p.a p.x

  at : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  at (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (a) (x) =
    let p = compose-down segs ch d a x
    in to-rider p.a p.x pose.along pose.across pose.yaw pose.hw

Section: Mappers

 How to express a point authored in SOME segment's frame in the rider's. A forward
 joint maps both of its segments through the chain -- `at` at index d, above.

 THE JOINT JUST BEHIND THE RIDER IS THE ONE THAT CANNOT, and it is the reason this
 abstraction exists at all. Its `from` is the PREVIOUS segment, which is not on the
 chain: the chain starts at the rider's current segment and only ever runs forward.
 So a point authored there steps FORWARD through the join with cur-to-next -- the
 inverse of what every other point does -- and then goes straight to the rider.
 Without that pass the pavement the rider is still crossing vanishes the instant
 his segment index flips, which is the bug the behind-joint pass was written for
 and which the tower and the corner creatures each had in turn.

 The zig's Mapper is a struct with a two-valued tag; the port keeps the struct and
 narrows the tag to a Boolean, because a Codex variant carries no payload and the
 four prev fields have to live somewhere. `is-chain` reads as the zig's
 `kind == .chain`.

  Mapper = record {
    is-chain : Boolean,
    d : Integer,
    prev-len : Real,
    prev-angle : Real,
    prev-right : Boolean,
    prev-w : Real
  }

 The zig defaults every Mapper field and fills in the one or two that matter. Codex
 records are built whole, so the two constructors below take that place -- and they
 also make the two kinds impossible to half-build, which the defaults did not.

  chain-map : Integer -> Mapper
  chain-map (d) =
    Mapper { is-chain = True, d = d, prev-len = 0.0, prev-angle = 0.0, prev-right = False, prev-w = 0.0 }

  prev-map : Segment -> Mapper
  prev-map (s) =
    Mapper { is-chain = False, d = 0, prev-len = s.length, prev-angle = s.exit-angle, prev-right = s.exit-right, prev-w = s.width }

  map-pt : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  map-pt (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (a) (x) =
    if m.is-chain
      then at segs ch pose m.d a x
      else let p = cur-to-next a x m.prev-len m.prev-angle m.prev-right m.prev-w
        in to-rider p.a p.x pose.along pose.across pose.yaw pose.hw

Section: Collection Limits and Culls

 Beyond this depth a tree draws as flat triangles rather than cone hulls, and the
 crown's foliage gradient fades to solid over this much depth. Both are drawing
 decisions, carried here because the collector is what measures the depth.

  detail-dist : Real
  detail-dist = 200.0

  crown-shade-dist : Real
  crown-shade-dist = 80.0

 Scenery shorter than this on screen is skipped.

  min-scenery-px : Real
  min-scenery-px = 2.0

  min-critter-px : Real
  min-critter-px = 2.0

 THE TWO CULLS ARE DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT AND BOTH MUST STAY LIVE. The SEGMENT
 cull saves projecting a critter at all: trees hide the farm animals past a few
 segments, and the corner safari animals carry a little further before they are
 worth it. The SIZE cull then trims whatever is left down to a genuine speck. The
 zig counts each separately every frame so a gate can assert both still fire -- a
 reach so loose the size cull never fires, or so tight the segments do all the
 work, is a bug in the budget. Those two counters are `pub`, and they are this
 chapter's only observable output.

  farm-seg-reach : Integer
  farm-seg-reach = 3

  safari-seg-reach : Integer
  safari-seg-reach = 5

 THE SIZE CULL MEASURES AGAINST THE STABLE BASE FOCAL, NOT THE LIVE ONE, and that
 is not an oversight to tidy up. The focal pulls in to 0.35x during a lean, so
 culling a critter on the live focal would yank close animals out of view the
 instant the rider turns and pop them back when he straightened. Scenery -- trees
 and the cat -- does cull on the live `cam-focal`. The two are different on
 purpose and the port keeps them different.

 The fixed-array caps. Codex lists carry their own length, so these bound nothing
 here; they are ported because they are observable IF reached, and the truncation
 below is where that would show. On this route they are not reached -- render's
 own note puts the tree peak at about 37 percent of its cap -- so the port
 truncates at the end where the zig stops mid-segment. Those differ only once a
 cap is hit, and making them agree there would mean threading a running count
 through every collector for a case this route cannot produce.

  max-vis-trees : Integer
  max-vis-trees = 640

  max-vis-towers : Integer
  max-vis-towers = 16

  max-vis-critters : Integer
  max-vis-critters = 320

  max-vis-cats : Integer
  max-vis-cats = 8

Section: Tower Placement

 Out past the corner, off to the right, and yawed. A long segment also stands its
 own tower halfway down, that far LEFT of the centreline.

  tower-beyond : Real
  tower-beyond = 160.0

  tower-right : Real
  tower-right = 20.0

  seg-tower-left : Real
  seg-tower-left = 100.0

 THE ZIG WRITES ITS OWN PI HERE, TRUNCATED, and the port copies the literal rather
 than reaching for Trig's `deg`. render.zig says `30.0 * 3.14159265 / 180.0`, which
 is 0.523598775 where thirty times a full-precision degree is 0.5235987756. The
 difference is below f32 and would almost certainly vanish at the seam -- but a
 port that silently improves a constant is a port whose disagreements no longer
 mean what they say, so the truncation is kept.

  tower-yaw : Real
  tower-yaw = 30.0 * 3.14159265 / 180.0

Section: The Collected Kinds

 The zig collects into PARALLEL ARRAYS -- t-right, t-fwd, t-h, t-col -- because it
 has to; the port collects records, which is the same data with the index made
 implicit. What the port does NOT change is that each kind is collected into its
 own list and merged only at the sort, because that is what makes the sort one
 pass over everything rather than a sort per kind.

  TreeItem = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    color : Integer
  }

 A tower is collected as the RECIPE for its base rather than as points: the mapper
 that owns its frame, its base centre and yaw, and the beacon's blink offset. It
 has to be, because the four base corners are re-derived at draw time from the
 yaw. `fwd` is the depth of the centre, which is what it sorts by.

  TowerItem = record {
    map : Mapper,
    a0 : Real,
    x0 : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    off : Real
  }

 Every emoji animal -- cows, bulls, the pig herd, the corner safari pair, the pond
 ducks -- collects to the same billboard, which is why one list holds all of them.

  Billboard = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    cp : Integer,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

 A placement carries its own verdict rather than being dropped, so the two culls
 can be counted separately. `kept` says it survived both; `size-culled` says which
 one rejected it. The near-plane cull is SILENT in the zig -- it `continue`s
 without touching a counter -- so a placement can be neither kept nor size-culled,
 and that third state is the one the port has to preserve.

  Placed = record {
    b : Billboard,
    kept : Boolean,
    size-culled : Boolean
  }

Section: The Crossing Cat

 A cat is a billboard like the animals, but its across is not authored -- it comes
 out of the crossing clock, keyed on the rider's ROAD-ALONG GAP to it. So it needs
 something none of the other kinds do: how far down the route the chain segment it
 stands on begins.

 That gap starts NEGATIVE. The rider is already `along` into his own segment, so
 the start of chain[0] is behind him, and each further segment adds its length.
 The zig accumulates it as it walks; the port sums it, which is the same number
 and does not need the walk to carry state.

  chain-gap : List Segment, List Integer, Real, Integer -> Real
  chain-gap (w) (ch) (along) (d) =
    if d <= 0 then 0.0 - along
    else chain-gap w ch along (d - 1) + (list-at w (list-at ch (d - 1))).length

  CatItem = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    pose-idx : Integer,
    lift : Real
  }

 THE CAT CULLS ON THE LIVE FOCAL, like the trees and unlike the critters, and it
 culls only at the near plane besides. render.zig argues the point: it is a flat
 billboard at one depth, so as the rider passes, the whole still scales and slides
 off to the left together -- it never streaks across the viewport the way a tower
 can -- and culling it earlier would vanish the collapsed cat exactly as he
 reaches it, instead of letting him pass it.

  cat-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Segment, Real -> List CatItem
  cat-item (w) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (gap) (sg) (v) =
    let st = cat-state sg.cat (gap + sg.cat.along) v
    in let rp = at w ch pose d sg.cat.along (st.across + sg.width / 2.0)
    in if rp.forward <= near then []
      else if sg.cat.height / rp.forward * cf < min-scenery-px then []
      else [CatItem { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = sg.cat.height, pose-idx = st.pose-idx, lift = st.lift }]

  seg-cat : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real -> List CatItem
  seg-cat (w) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (along) (v) =
    let sg = list-at w (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.has-cat then cat-item w ch pose d cf (chain-gap w ch along d) sg v else []

  walk-cats : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List CatItem
  walk-cats (w) (ch) (pose) (cf) (along) (v) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-cat w ch pose d cf along v) & walk-cats w ch pose cf along v (d + 1)

Section: Placing Billboards

 One rule for every emoji animal: map it into the rider frame, drop it if it is
 behind the near plane, size-cull it if it would project shorter than the floor.

  no-billboard : Billboard
  no-billboard = Billboard { right = 0.0, fwd = 0.0, height = 0.0, cp = 0, face-right = False }

  verdict : RiderPt, Real, Integer, Boolean -> Placed
  verdict (rp) (h) (cp) (fr) =
    if rp.forward <= near
      then Placed { b = no-billboard, kept = False, size-culled = False }
      else if h / rp.forward * focal < min-critter-px
        then Placed { b = no-billboard, kept = False, size-culled = True }
        else Placed { b = Billboard { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = h, cp = cp, face-right = fr }, kept = True, size-culled = False }

  place-critter : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Critter -> Placed
  place-critter (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) (cr) =
    let rp = at segs ch pose d cr.along (cr.across + hw)
    in verdict rp cr.height cr.codepoint cr.face-right

 The behind joint's critters map FORWARD through the join instead, so they take
 the mapper rather than a chain index. Same verdict.

  place-critter-via : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Critter -> Placed
  place-critter-via (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (hw) (cr) =
    let rp = map-pt segs ch pose m cr.along (cr.across + hw)
    in verdict rp cr.height cr.codepoint cr.face-right

 A DUCK IS AUTHORED IN THE CORNER FRAME AND MAPS RAW -- no half-width shift. Its
 `cu` is the bottom-left across directly, which is why the pond's negative cu sits
 LEFT of the left edge rather than left of the centre line. Getting this wrong
 would slide the whole pond half a lane and still look like a pond.

  place-duck : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Duck -> Placed
  place-duck (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (dk) =
    let rp = map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len + dk.p.cv) dk.p.cu
    in verdict rp duck-height duck-codepoint dk.face-right

Section: Harvesting a Placement List

 Collect the survivors and count the size-culled, over a list of placements. Two
 walks over one list rather than one walk returning a pair, because a pair would
 need a record whose only purpose is to be taken apart again.

  kept-of : List Placed, Integer -> List Billboard
  kept-of (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else if (list-at ps i).kept then [(list-at ps i).b] & kept-of ps (i + 1)
    else kept-of ps (i + 1)

  size-culled-of : List Placed, Integer -> Integer
  size-culled-of (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else if (list-at ps i).size-culled then 1 + size-culled-of ps (i + 1)
    else size-culled-of ps (i + 1)

  place-all : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, List Critter, Integer -> List Placed
  place-all (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) (crs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length crs then []
    else [place-critter segs ch pose d hw (list-at crs i)] & place-all segs ch pose d hw crs (i + 1)

  place-all-via : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, List Critter, Integer -> List Placed
  place-all-via (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (hw) (crs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length crs then []
    else [place-critter-via segs ch pose m hw (list-at crs i)] & place-all-via segs ch pose m hw crs (i + 1)

  place-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Integer -> List Placed
  place-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ducks then []
    else [place-duck segs ch pose m from-len (list-at ducks i)] & place-ducks segs ch pose m from-len (i + 1)

Section: Collecting Trees

 Trees are authored centre-relative, so the half width shifts them to
 from-the-left. They cull on the LIVE focal, unlike the critters above.

  place-tree : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Tree -> List TreeItem
  place-tree (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (hw) (tr) =
    let rp = at segs ch pose d tr.along (tr.across + hw)
    in if rp.forward <= near then []
      else if tr.height / rp.forward * cf < min-scenery-px then []
      else [TreeItem { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = tr.height, color = tr.color }]

  seg-trees : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, List Tree, Integer -> List TreeItem
  seg-trees (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (hw) (trs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length trs then []
    else (place-tree segs ch pose d cf hw (list-at trs i)) & seg-trees segs ch pose d cf hw trs (i + 1)

Section: Collecting Towers

 A tower is collected only if its base CENTRE is in front of the near plane, and
 the centre is what it sorts by. The exit tower is keyed off its own segment; the
 mid-tower adds sixty so a long segment's two towers do not blink in step.

  tower-if-ahead : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List TowerItem
  tower-if-ahead (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (a0) (x0) (yw) (key) =
    let c = map-pt segs ch pose m a0 x0
    in if c.forward <= near then []
      else [TowerItem { map = m, a0 = a0, x0 = x0, yaw = yw, fwd = c.forward, off = beacon-offset-for key }]

  seg-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  seg-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in (tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (chain-map d) (sg.length + tower-beyond) (sg.width / 2.0 + tower-right) tower-yaw (list-at ch d))
       & (seg-mid-tower segs ch pose d)

  seg-mid-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  seg-mid-tower (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.has-mid-tower
      then tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (chain-map d) (sg.length / 2.0) (sg.width / 2.0 - seg-tower-left) 0.0 (list-at ch d + 60)
      else []

Section: Collecting Critters

  is-pond : Creature -> Boolean
  is-pond (c) =
   when c
    is DuckPond -> True
    is NoCreature -> False
    is Elephant -> False
    is Giraffe -> False
    is Zebra -> False
    is Rhino -> False

 The farm animals of one chain segment, if it is near enough to be worth
 projecting at all. Cows first, then the pig herd -- the order the zig fills its
 array in, which is the order ties break in at the sort.

  seg-farm : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real -> List Placed
  seg-farm (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= farm-seg-reach then []
      else (place-all segs ch pose d hw sg.cows 0) & (place-all segs ch pose d hw sg.pigs 0)

 The SEGMENT cull's tally: every farm animal on a chain segment beyond the reach,
 counted without being placed. This is the counter that makes the chain's own
 extent observable from outside render.zig, because it sums over exactly the chain
 indices at or past the reach.

  seg-cull-count : List Segment, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  seg-cull-count (segs) (ch) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= farm-seg-reach then list-length sg.cows + list-length sg.pigs else 0

 The corner pair at this segment's exit. Skipped at the terminus, which has no
 exit corner, and beyond the safari reach.

  seg-safari : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real -> List Placed
  seg-safari (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.terminates then []
      else if d >= safari-seg-reach then []
      else place-all segs ch pose d hw (corner-critters sg.exit-creature sg.length sg.exit-right hw) 0

 The three ducks on the pond at this segment's exit. The pond's WATER and BANK are
 ground quads and belong to the paint pass, not to this one; only the billboards
 are collected for the sort.

  seg-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  seg-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= safari-seg-reach then []
      else if is-pond sg.exit-creature then place-ducks segs ch pose (chain-map d) sg.length 0
      else []

 Everything one chain segment contributes to the billboard list, in the zig's own
 fill order: farm animals, then the corner pair, then the ducks.

  seg-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  seg-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let hw = (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0
    in (seg-farm segs ch pose d hw) & (seg-safari segs ch pose d hw) & (seg-ducks segs ch pose d)

Section: Walking the Chain

  walk-trees : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> List TreeItem
  walk-trees (segs) (ch) (pose) (cf) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-trees segs ch pose d cf ((list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).trees 0)
         & walk-trees segs ch pose cf (d + 1)

  walk-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  walk-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-towers segs ch pose d) & walk-towers segs ch pose (d + 1)

  walk-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  walk-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-billboards segs ch pose d) & walk-billboards segs ch pose (d + 1)

  walk-seg-cull : List Segment, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  walk-seg-cull (segs) (ch) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then 0
    else seg-cull-count segs ch d + walk-seg-cull segs ch (d + 1)

Section: The Joint Just Behind

 The segment the rider just left owns a corner, and everything on it -- pavement,
 tower, creatures, pond -- pops out the instant his segment index flips unless it
 is drawn through the join. The route is finite, so there is a previous segment
 everywhere EXCEPT the start.

 THE BEHIND CRITTERS TAKE NO REACH TEST. The forward ones are gated on
 safari-seg-reach; this pair is one join away by construction, so the zig does not
 test it and neither does the port.

  behind-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  behind-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in (place-all-via segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (pv.width / 2.0) (corner-critters pv.exit-creature pv.length pv.exit-right (pv.width / 2.0)) 0)
       & (behind-ducks segs ch pose pv)

  behind-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Segment -> List Placed
  behind-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (pv) =
    if is-pond pv.exit-creature then place-ducks segs ch pose (prev-map pv) pv.length 0 else []

  behind-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  behind-tower (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (pv.length + tower-beyond) (pv.width / 2.0 + tower-right) tower-yaw prev-idx

Section: The Corner's Outer Shoulders

 Both the corner pavement and the guard rail hang off the same three points: each
 segment's OUTER shoulder at the join, and the apex Q where the two extended
 shoulders cross. Which edge is outer flips with the turn -- a right turn's outer
 edge is the left one.

  outer-cu : Boolean, Real -> Real
  outer-cu (exit-right) (wd) = if exit-right then 0.0 else wd

 Q is found by extending each shoulder one metre past the join and intersecting
 the two lines. A real turn has a non-zero angle, so they are never parallel.

  joint-apex : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> RiderPt
  joint-apex (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let fcu = outer-cu exit-right from-w
    in let tx = outer-cu exit-right to-w
    in line-meet (map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu) (map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len + 1.0) fcu) (map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 tx) (map-pt segs ch pose to-map 1.0 tx)

Section: The Guard Rail Path

 Append points from `from` EXCLUSIVE to `to` inclusive at about one metre spacing
 -- one rail post per metre along a straight leg of the corner. At least one step
 always, so a zero-length leg still lands its endpoint.

  leg-steps : Real -> Integer
  leg-steps (dist) =
    let r = round-real dist
    in real-to-int (if r < 1.0 then 1.0 else r)

  leg-points : RiderPt, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List RiderPt
  leg-points (from) (dr) (df) (steps) (i) =
    if i > steps then []
    else let t = real-from-int i / real-from-int steps
    in [RiderPt { right = from.right + dr * t, forward = from.forward + df * t }] & leg-points from dr df steps (i + 1)

  push-leg : RiderPt, RiderPt -> List RiderPt
  push-leg (from) (to) =
    let dr = to.right - from.right
    in let df = to.forward - from.forward
    in leg-points from dr df (leg-steps (real-sqrt (dr * dr + df * df))) 1

 The run-up counts DOWN to the end edge, so the last point of the run-up is the
 outer corner itself and the first leg starts from it.

  rail-run-up : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  rail-run-up (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (cu) (k) =
    if k < 0 then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len - real-from-int k) cu] & rail-run-up segs ch pose m from-len cu (k - 1)

  rail-run-out : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  rail-run-out (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (x) (k) =
    if k > rail-runout then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (real-from-int k) x] & rail-run-out segs ch pose m x (k + 1)

 The whole corner path: run-up along the from segment's outer shoulder, the two
 legs into and out of the apex, and the run-out along the to segment's. guard_rail
 then raises that path to a bar strip and its posts.

  joint-rail-path : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RiderPt
  joint-rail-path (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let fcu = outer-cu exit-right from-w
    in let tx = outer-cu exit-right to-w
    in let of-pt = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu
    in let ot-pt = map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 tx
    in let q = joint-apex segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right
    in (rail-run-up segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu rail-runout) & (push-leg of-pt q) & (push-leg q ot-pt) & (rail-run-out segs ch pose to-map tx 1)

  joint-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RailPoly
  joint-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    rail-emit (joint-rail-path segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right)

 Every FORWARD joint on the chain, in chain order, then the joint behind. That is
 the order the zig's rail store holds them in, and the order ties break in.

  walk-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  walk-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d + 1 >= list-length ch then []
    else (joint-rails segs ch pose (chain-map d) (chain-map (d + 1)) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width (list-at segs (list-at ch (d + 1))).width (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).exit-right)
         & walk-rails segs ch pose (d + 1)

  behind-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  behind-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in joint-rails segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (chain-map 0) pv.length pv.width (list-at segs (list-at ch 0)).width pv.exit-right

Section: The Chased Truck

 Walk the truck's LEAD -- its route distance minus the rider's -- forward along
 the chain to find which segment its centre lands on. It sorts at that centre-line
 depth, one item however long the body is.

  TruckAt = record {
    present : Boolean,
    d : Integer,
    along : Real,
    fwd : Real
  }

  no-truck : TruckAt
  no-truck = TruckAt { present = False, d = 0, along = 0.0, fwd = 0.0 }

  truck-step : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> TruckAt
  truck-step (segs) (ch) (pose) (remaining) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then no-truck
    else if remaining > (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length
      then truck-step segs ch pose (remaining - (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length) (d + 1)
      else truck-here segs ch pose remaining d

  truck-here : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> TruckAt
  truck-here (segs) (ch) (pose) (remaining) (d) =
    let c = at segs ch pose d remaining ((list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0)
    in if c.forward > near
      then TruckAt { present = True, d = d, along = remaining, fwd = c.forward }
      else no-truck

 A lead of zero or less means the truck is level with or behind the rider, and it
 is simply not drawn -- the zig does not clamp it forward.

  truck-at : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real -> TruckAt
  truck-at (segs) (ch) (pose) (along) (lead) =
    if lead > 0.0 then truck-step segs ch pose (along + lead) 0 else no-truck

Section: The Depth Sort

 One order over everything that stands up off the ground, far to near, so a tree
 occludes a rail and a rail occludes a cow by honest depth rather than by paint
 order. This is what guard_rail's collect-then-sort design exists to feed, and why
 its RailPoly carries a `fwd` it never draws with.

  Kind =
   | KTree
   | KTower
   | KCow
   | KCat
   | KTruck
   | KRail

  Item = record {
    fwd : Real,
    kind : Kind,
    i : Integer
  }

  tree-items : List TreeItem, Integer -> List Item
  tree-items (ts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at ts i).fwd, kind = KTree, i = i }] & tree-items ts (i + 1)

  tower-items : List TowerItem, Integer -> List Item
  tower-items (ts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at ts i).fwd, kind = KTower, i = i }] & tower-items ts (i + 1)

  cow-items : List Billboard, Integer -> List Item
  cow-items (bs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at bs i).fwd, kind = KCow, i = i }] & cow-items bs (i + 1)

  cat-items : List CatItem, Integer -> List Item
  cat-items (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at cs i).fwd, kind = KCat, i = i }] & cat-items cs (i + 1)

  rail-items : List RailPoly, Integer -> List Item
  rail-items (rs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length rs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at rs i).fwd, kind = KRail, i = i }] & rail-items rs (i + 1)

  truck-items : TruckAt -> List Item
  truck-items (t) = if t.present then [Item { fwd = t.fwd, kind = KTruck, i = 0 }] else []

 THE SORT MUST BE STABLE AND THE ZIG'S IS. Its insertion loop moves a key left
 only while the element before it is STRICTLY nearer-to-far -- `items[j-1].fwd <
 key.fwd` -- so equal depths keep the order they were merged in. That matters more
 here than it looks: a rail bar and its post share a corner and can land on the
 same depth, and so can two roadside trees on opposite shoulders.

 THE PORT SORTS BY MERGING RATHER THAN BY INSERTING, and that is the one place in
 this chapter where the algorithm departs from the zig on purpose. A stable merge
 sort and a stable insertion sort produce the SAME total order -- same comparison,
 same tie rule -- so nothing observable changes. What changes is the cost, and on
 persistent lists the difference is not a constant factor.

 Insertion sort on a mutable array shifts elements in place. On a Codex list there
 is nothing to shift: rebuilding the tail past the insertion point allocates a list
 per element, so ONE insert is quadratic in allocations and the whole sort is
 cubic. Measured, it is not slow but fatal -- seven hundred items across eight
 frames exhausted codexzig's whole 4 GiB bump heap, which never reclaims (C6). The
 merge is quadratic in copies and comes in around a hundred megabytes.

 So the rule this is an instance of: PORTING AN ALGORITHM FAITHFULLY MEANS PORTING
 ITS ANSWER, NOT ITS COST MODEL. An in-place algorithm carried literally onto
 persistent lists can gain a whole complexity class, and the bump heap turns that
 into a crash rather than a wait. See PORTING_NOTES E4.

  rest-from : List Item, Integer -> List Item
  rest-from (ys) (j) =
    if j >= list-length ys then []
    else [list-at ys j] & rest-from ys (j + 1)

 Merge two runs already in far-to-near order. `a` wins ties -- the test asks
 whether b is STRICTLY deeper -- which is what makes the merge stable and matches
 the zig's strict `<`.

  merge-items : List Item, List Item, Integer, Integer -> List Item
  merge-items (a) (b) (i) (j) =
    if i >= list-length a then rest-from b j
    else if j >= list-length b then rest-from a i
    else if deeper-than (list-at b j).fwd (list-at a i).fwd
      then [list-at b j] & merge-items a b i (j + 1)
      else [list-at a i] & merge-items a b (i + 1) j

 THE SORT DECLINES TO ORDER TWO DEPTHS IT CANNOT TELL APART, and that is the one
 place this chapter's comparison is not the zig's character for character. The
 zig's key is an f32; the port's is a Real, which is f64 in every plug. Two depths
 that differ by less than the f32 grid are THE SAME NUMBER in the game -- its sort
 sees a tie and keeps merge order -- while the port can see a difference and would
 order them by it. Sorting on precision the oracle does not have is not fidelity;
 it is the port inventing an order.

 So `deeper-than` is a strict comparison with half an f32 ulp of slack, and the
 slack is measured rather than chosen. A rail post and a rail bar in the whole
 frame check land within 6e-8 of each other and swap without it; at 2e-8 they
 still swap, so the gap is between a sixth and a half of an ulp. Half an ulp is
 the largest threshold that cannot tie two depths the game holds apart, since two
 distinct f32s differ by a whole one.

 A grader that measures inversions has to use the same rule -- RenderCheck's does,
 and its own prose says why. A property asserted at a finer resolution than the
 sort works at reports the rule as a defect.

  sort-tie : Real
  sort-tie = 0.00000006

  deeper-than : Real, Real -> Boolean
  deeper-than (x) (y) = x - y > sort-tie * real-max (real-abs y) 1.0

  sort-items : List Item -> List Item
  sort-items (xs) =
    if list-length xs <= 1 then xs
    else merge-items (sort-items (list-take xs (list-length xs / 2))) (sort-items (list-drop xs (list-length xs / 2))) 0 0

Section: The Frame's Collection

 Everything the chain walk gathers, plus the two cull tallies and the one depth
 order over the lot. What this does NOT carry is the ground -- the road strips,
 the corner pavement and the pond's water and bank are painted inside the same
 walk in the zig, but they are quads on the floor and never enter the sort, so
 they belong to the paint pass and are not ported here.

 ALL SEVEN KINDS ARE COLLECTED NOW. The cat was the last, and what unblocked it
 was not writing the logarithm its `pow(b, 0.7)` seemed to need: 0.75 is dyadic
 where 0.7 is not, so the front-loaded leap is a pair of square roots and the
 approximation is bounded at 8.6 cm for two frames. Safari chapter Cat argues it.

  Collected = record {
    trees : List TreeItem,
    towers : List TowerItem,
    cows : List Billboard,
    cats : List CatItem,
    rails : List RailPoly,
    truck : TruckAt,
    order : List Item,
    cull-seg : Integer,
    cull-size : Integer
  }

  prev-index : Integer -> Integer
  prev-index (seg-idx) = if seg-idx > 0 then seg-idx - 1 else 0

  all-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  all-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-towers segs ch pose 0) & (behind-tower segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-towers segs ch pose 0

  all-placed : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  all-placed (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-billboards segs ch pose 0) & (behind-billboards segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-billboards segs ch pose 0

  all-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  all-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-rails segs ch pose 0) & (behind-rails segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-rails segs ch pose 0

 The merge order is the zig's own array order -- trees, towers, billboards, the
 truck, then the rails -- and since the sort is stable it is also the order equal
 depths come out in.

  collect : List Segment, Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Collected
  collect (segs) (seg-idx) (pose) (cf) (along) (v) (truck-pos) =
    let ch = build-chain segs seg-idx
    in let placed = all-placed segs ch pose seg-idx
    in let trees = list-take (walk-trees segs ch pose cf 0) max-vis-trees
    in let towers = list-take (all-towers segs ch pose seg-idx) max-vis-towers
    in let cows = list-take (kept-of placed 0) max-vis-critters
    in let cats = list-take (walk-cats segs ch pose cf along v 0) max-vis-cats
    in let rails = all-rails segs ch pose seg-idx
    in let tk = truck-at segs ch pose along (truck-pos - route-distance segs seg-idx along)
    in Collected {
      trees = trees,
      towers = towers,
      cows = cows,
      cats = cats,
      rails = rails,
      truck = tk,
      order = sort-items ((tree-items trees 0) & (tower-items towers 0) & (cow-items cows 0) & (cat-items cats 0) & (truck-items tk) & (rail-items rails 0)),
      cull-seg = walk-seg-cull segs ch 0,
      cull-size = size-culled-of placed 0
    }

Section: The Ground

 The road surface and everything paved: road strips, each corner's approach and
 pavement, and the pond's water and bank. These are quads on the FLOOR. They never
 enter the depth sort -- they are painted first, in walk order, and everything that
 stands up is sorted and painted over them.

  road-color : Integer
  road-color = 3421500

 Road strips are sliced this long so a bend reads smooth rather than as one long
 flat quad cutting the curve.

  road-chunk : Real
  road-chunk = 25.0

 Metres of approach road a joint paints behind its end edge. It is what covers the
 joint when the joint is the one BEHIND the rider.

  entry-road-dist : Real
  entry-road-dist = 40.0

 A ground vertex drops below the rider's tangent plane by the curvature at its
 horizontal distance, which is what bends the road toward a finite horizon instead
 of a vanishing point at infinity. The sign is the thing to get right: ground-drop
 returns a positive distance and the height is its NEGATIVE.

  ground-vert : RiderPt -> Vec3
  ground-vert (p) =
    Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = 0.0 - ground-drop p.right p.forward }

  ground-verts : List RiderPt, Integer -> List Vec3
  ground-verts (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [ground-vert (list-at ps i)] & ground-verts ps (i + 1)

 THE EIGHT-POINT GUARD IS THE ZIG'S FIXED BUFFER AND IT IS KEPT. A caller handing
 in more than eight points gets nothing at all rather than a truncated polygon,
 which is the zig's behaviour and is the safer of the two: a silently shortened
 ground quad would be a wrong shape painted confidently.

  emit-ground-color : List RiderPt, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-ground-color (ps) (color) (cf) (view-w) =
    if list-length ps > 8 then []
    else ground-clip (clip-near (ground-verts ps 0) near) color cf view-w

  ground-clip : List Vec3, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  ground-clip (vs) (color) (cf) (view-w) =
    if list-length vs < 3 then []
    else push-poly color (project-all vs cf view-w 0)

  emit-ground : List RiderPt, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-ground (ps) (cf) (view-w) = emit-ground-color ps road-color cf view-w

Section: The Road Strip

 The zig takes the ceiling of length over the chunk size and floors it at one
 chunk. Codex's foreword has a floor and no ceiling, so this is the identity
 ceil x = -floor(-x) rather than a second rounding rule.

  ceil-real : Real -> Real
  ceil-real (x) = 0.0 - floor-real (0.0 - x)

  chunks-for : Real -> Integer
  chunks-for (len) =
    let c = ceil-real (len / road-chunk)
    in real-to-int (if c < 1.0 then 1.0 else c)

 EACH SLICE IS CUT FROM THE SEGMENT LENGTH BY INDEX, not accumulated by adding a
 chunk at a time. It matters: the zig computes a0 and a1 as length*i/n and
 length*(i+1)/n, so the last slice ends exactly on the segment end and the strip
 cannot drift short of the joint by an accumulated rounding.

  road-slice : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  road-slice (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) (ci) (n) (len) (wd) =
    if ci >= n then []
    else (emit-ground (slice-quad segs ch pose d ci n len wd) cf view-w)
         & road-slice segs ch pose d cf view-w (ci + 1) n len wd

  slice-quad : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> List RiderPt
  slice-quad (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ci) (n) (len) (wd) =
    let a0 = len * real-from-int ci / real-from-int n
    in let a1 = len * real-from-int (ci + 1) / real-from-int n
    in [at segs ch pose d a0 0.0, at segs ch pose d a0 wd, at segs ch pose d a1 wd, at segs ch pose d a1 0.0]

  seg-road : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-road (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in road-slice segs ch pose d cf view-w 0 (chunks-for sg.length) sg.length sg.width

Section: A Joint's Ground

 The approach road is the `from` segment's tail, its end edge drawn back
 entry-road-dist. The corner pavement is a quad from the INNER fuse-corner out to
 the outer apex Q: inner corner, `from`'s outer corner, Q, `to`'s outer corner.

 THE INNER CORNER IS THE EDGE THE TURN GOES TOWARD and the outer is the one it
 goes away from, so both flip with the turn direction and they flip oppositely.
 Getting the pair the wrong way round still paints a quad -- just one folded
 through the intersection.

  joint-approach : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real -> List RiderPt
  joint-approach (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (from-len) (from-w) =
    [map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len 0.0,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len from-w,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len - entry-road-dist) from-w,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len - entry-road-dist) 0.0]

  joint-pavement : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RiderPt
  joint-pavement (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let inner = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len (if exit-right then from-w else 0.0)
    in let outer-from = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len (outer-cu exit-right from-w)
    in let outer-to = map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 (outer-cu exit-right to-w)
    in let q = joint-apex segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right
    in [inner, outer-from, q, outer-to]

  emit-joint-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-joint-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) (cf) (view-w) =
    (emit-ground (joint-approach segs ch pose from-map from-len from-w) cf view-w)
    & (emit-ground (joint-pavement segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right) cf view-w)

Section: The Pond's Ground

 The water then the bank, authored in the incoming corner frame: corner (cu, cv)
 is map (from-len + cv, cu). The ducks that float on it are billboards and were
 collected for the depth sort; only the two ground shapes are painted here.

  pond-shape : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, List PondPt, Integer -> List RiderPt
  pond-shape (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len + (list-at ps i).cv) (list-at ps i).cu] & pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len ps (i + 1)

  emit-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (cf) (view-w) =
    (emit-ground-color (pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len water-outline 0) water-color cf view-w)
    & (emit-ground-color (pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len bank 0) bank-color cf view-w)

Section: Walking the Ground

 One chain segment's floor: its road strip, then its exit joint's ground when the
 next segment is in view, then the pond at that exit if there is one. The pond
 ground takes NO reach test -- the ducks on it do, but the water is painted
 wherever the corner is drawn at all.

  seg-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    (seg-road segs ch pose d cf view-w)
    & (seg-joint-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w)
    & (seg-pond-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w)

  seg-joint-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-joint-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    if d + 1 >= list-length ch then []
    else emit-joint-ground segs ch pose (chain-map d) (chain-map (d + 1)) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width (list-at segs (list-at ch (d + 1))).width (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).exit-right cf view-w

  seg-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if is-pond sg.exit-creature
      then emit-pond-ground segs ch pose (chain-map d) sg.length cf view-w
      else []

  walk-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  walk-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (cf) (view-w) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w) & walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w (d + 1)

  behind-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  behind-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) (cf) (view-w) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in (emit-joint-ground segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (chain-map 0) pv.length pv.width (list-at segs (list-at ch 0)).width pv.exit-right cf view-w)
       & (behind-pond-ground segs ch pose pv cf view-w)

  behind-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Segment, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  behind-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (pv) (cf) (view-w) =
    if is-pond pv.exit-creature
      then emit-pond-ground segs ch pose (prev-map pv) pv.length cf view-w
      else []

 The whole floor for one frame, forward chain then the joint behind.

  frame-ground : List Segment, Integer, Pose, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-ground (segs) (seg-idx) (pose) (cf) (view-w) =
    let ch = build-chain segs seg-idx
    in if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w 0) & (behind-ground segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1) cf view-w)
      else walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w 0

Chapter: TruckBody
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Render

 The trailer, the cab, the tires, the headlight beams and the brake lights: the
 truck as it is SEEN, where `Safari chapter Truck` is the truck as it MOVES.

 A port of wasm/truck.zig's `drawBody` and the three private drawers under it.
 SPLIT FROM Truck FOR THE SAME REASON CatDraw IS SPLIT FROM Cat, and the split is
 the zig's own seam read twice. Truck's motion half reaches nothing but the world
 and a few of the rider's constants, so World can cite it and every check that
 touches the world pays five constants for it. This half reaches `render.at`, the
 whole chain composition, the camera and the paint wire -- so it cites Render, and
 anything citing it pays for the mapping spine. Keeping them apart is what lets
 TruckCheck stay a nine-case motion oracle while this one grades a draw.

 IT IS THE LAST UNPORTED DRAWING CODE IN THE GAME. What unblocked it was Paint's
 tag 4: the beams and the brake glow are radial-gradient polygons, and until Paint
 modelled that tag there was no honest way to emit them.

 THE ZIG'S OWN COMMENTS SAY THE BEAMS AND THE GLOW ARE DEFERRED, AND THEY ARE
 STALE. drawBody's header and the brake-light block both claim the wedges and the
 halo still need "a gradient/alpha seam tag the polygon-only blitter doesn't have
 yet", and the file's top comment still calls the truck a placeholder blue dot.
 The CODE calls drawWedge and drawBrakeGlow and pushes tag 4 for both. The code is
 the oracle -- the port follows what runs, not what the comment says, and the
 check would have caught the difference either way, because a missing wedge is two
 missing commands and structure is graded exactly.

 EVERY VERTEX GOES THROUGH `at` AND THEN DROPS BY THE GROUND CURVE. That is `p3`
 in the zig, and it is the whole reason the truck reads as sitting on the road
 rather than floating above it: the body is authored in the segment-local frame of
 whichever chain index its centre landed on -- along the road, across from the
 left edge, metres up -- and the fake-horizon curvature is subtracted at the end,
 per vertex, so a long trailer bends with the road it is standing on.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 Metres, and all of them mirror truck.ts's buildTruck. The trailer is narrower
 than the four-metre lane so it can round a corner without clipping the rails.

  truck-length : Real
  truck-length = 8.4

  truck-width : Real
  truck-width = 2.4

  truck-height : Real
  truck-height = 3.6

 The cab sticks this far out in front of the trailer, and its roof is this
 fraction of it -- the windshield rakes down over the rest.

  cab-length : Real
  cab-length = 3.5

  cab-roof-frac : Real
  cab-roof-frac = 0.3

  tire-radius : Real
  tire-radius = 0.5

 The trailer floor sits just above the tire tops; the cab floor sits at the tire
 centre line, which is the step the side silhouette walks up.

  trailer-bottom : Real
  trailer-bottom = 2.0 * tire-radius + 0.12

  cab-bottom : Real
  cab-bottom = tire-radius

  tire-pair-gap : Real
  tire-pair-gap = 1.2

  trailer-axle-inset : Real
  trailer-axle-inset = 1.6

  cab-axle-frac : Real
  cab-axle-frac = 0.55

  tire-sides : Integer
  tire-sides = 16

Section: Colours

 Three shades of the same dark blue, which is what makes the prism read as solid
 rather than as a flat silhouette: the sides are darkest, the rear and nose are
 the body colour, the roof is lightest.

  body-color : Integer
  body-color = 1846886

  roof-color : Integer
  roof-color = 3822248

  side-color : Integer
  side-color = 1384784

  brake-color : Integer
  brake-color = 16722456

  tire-color : Integer
  tire-color = 1381658

Section: The Headlight Beams

 Two WEDGES of light thrown forward from the cab-front corners, each a flat 3D
 side-silhouette -- a slit at the truck face fanning open down the road -- filled
 with a radial glow from the lamp. Its 3D angle alone decides how it reads: broad
 in profile, a sliver head-on. Shown only once the sun is behind the mountains.

  headlight-h : Real
  headlight-h = 1.0

  headlight-inset : Real
  headlight-inset = 0.3

  cone-near-half : Real
  cone-near-half = 0.25

  cone-far-center : Real
  cone-far-center = 0.7

  cone-far-half : Real
  cone-far-half = 0.9

  cone-length : Real
  cone-length = 20.4

 THE TWO-COLOUR WORDS ARE 0xAARRGGBB, NOT 0xRRGGBB, and that is what makes a beam
 light rather than a white wedge painted on the road. Warm white 255,248,214 at
 alpha 0.765 in the core, the same white at 0.27 at the edge. Paint's tag 4 is the
 only command besides the beacon that carries alpha at all.

  beam-core : Integer
  beam-core = 3288332502

  beam-edge : Integer
  beam-edge = 1174403286

 The brake-light halo: red 255,80,60 at alpha 0.9 falling to 255,42,24 at zero.
 The edge stop being FULLY TRANSPARENT is the point -- the halo has no rim.

  glow-core : Integer
  glow-core = 3875491900

  glow-edge : Integer
  glow-edge = 16722456

  glow-sides : Integer
  glow-sides = 16

Section: A Body Point

 A point authored in the body's frame -- along the segment, across from its left
 edge, metres up -- mapped into the rider's frame and then lowered onto the curved
 ground. The drop is a POSITIVE distance and the height is `h` minus it, which is
 the same sign convention Render's ground pass states.

  truck-p3 : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real -> Vec3
  truck-p3 (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (along) (x) (h) =
    let r = at segs ch pose d along x
    in Vec3 { right = r.right, forward = r.forward, height = h - ground-drop r.right r.forward }

 The five abscissas and two edges every face is built from, computed once. `a0` is
 the trailer rear -- the face we chase -- and `a2` is the cab nose.

  TruckBox = record {
    a0 : Real,
    a1 : Real,
    a2 : Real,
    a-roof : Real,
    xl : Real,
    xr : Real
  }

  truck-box : Real, Real -> TruckBox
  truck-box (center-along) (hw) =
    let a0 = center-along - truck-length / 2.0
    in let a1 = center-along + truck-length / 2.0
    in TruckBox { a0 = a0, a1 = a1, a2 = a1 + cab-length, a-roof = a1 + cab-length * cab-roof-frac, xl = hw - truck-width / 2.0, xr = hw + truck-width / 2.0 }

Section: The Faces

 A face is a polygon in the rider frame plus the colour it fills with and the mean
 depth it sorts by. The zig recomputes that mean inside its sort's comparison;
 the port stores it, which is the same number and turns a repeated fold into one.

  TruckFace = record {
    color : Integer,
    fwd : Real,
    v : List Vec3
  }

  sum-fwd : List Vec3, Integer -> Real
  sum-fwd (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).forward + sum-fwd ps (i + 1)

  truck-face : Integer, List Vec3 -> TruckFace
  truck-face (color) (ps) =
    TruckFace { color = color, fwd = sum-fwd ps 0 / real-from-int (list-length ps), v = ps }

 EACH SIDE IS ONE SILHOUETTE, not a stack of quads, and walking it is the clearest
 statement of the truck's shape in the whole file: up the rear, along the roof,
 down the windshield and the nose, back along the lower cab floor, up the step to
 the higher trailer floor.

  truck-side : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real -> TruckFace
  truck-side (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (x) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 x trailer-bottom
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 x truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof x truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 x (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p4 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 x cab-bottom
    in let p5 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a1 x cab-bottom
    in let p6 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a1 x trailer-bottom
    in truck-face side-color [p0, p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6]

  truck-rear : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-rear (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl trailer-bottom
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr trailer-bottom
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl truck-height
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-roof : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-roof (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl truck-height
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xr truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xl truck-height
    in truck-face roof-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-windshield : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-windshield (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xl truck-height
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xr truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl (truck-height / 2.0)
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-nose : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-nose (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr cab-bottom
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl cab-bottom
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

Section: The Tires

 Five axles a side: a tandem pair inset from each trailer end and one under the
 cab. Each tire is a FLAT CIRCLE in its side plane -- no depth, no rim -- so it
 costs one polygon and still occludes correctly, because it joins the face sort.

  truck-axles : TruckBox -> List Real
  truck-axles (bx) =
    let rear = bx.a0 + trailer-axle-inset
    in let front = bx.a1 - trailer-axle-inset
    in let cab = bx.a1 + cab-length * cab-axle-frac
    in [rear - tire-pair-gap / 2.0, rear + tire-pair-gap / 2.0, front - tire-pair-gap / 2.0, front + tire-pair-gap / 2.0, cab]

 The circle is swept in the (along, height) plane at a fixed x, so it lies in the
 side of the body rather than facing the camera. Its centre is one tire radius up,
 which puts the bottom of every tire exactly on the road.

  tire-pt : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer -> Vec3
  tire-pt (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ac) (x) (i) =
    let th = real-from-int i / real-from-int tire-sides * two-pi
    in truck-p3 segs ch pose d (ac + tire-radius * r-cos th) x (tire-radius + tire-radius * r-sin th)

  tire-pts : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer -> List Vec3
  tire-pts (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ac) (x) (i) =
    if i >= tire-sides then []
    else [tire-pt segs ch pose d ac x i] & tire-pts segs ch pose d ac x (i + 1)

  side-tires : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, List Real, Real, Integer -> List TruckFace
  side-tires (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (axles) (x) (i) =
    if i >= list-length axles then []
    else [truck-face tire-color (tire-pts segs ch pose d (list-at axles i) x 0)] & side-tires segs ch pose d axles x (i + 1)

 SIXTEEN FACES, AND THE ORDER THEY ARE BUILT IN IS PART OF THE ANSWER. The sort
 below is stable, so two faces at the same mean depth stay in build order -- and
 the two sides are at the same depth whenever the truck is square-on to the eye,
 which is most of a straight. Build order is the zig's: both sides, then the rear,
 roof, windshield and nose, then the left tires and the right.

  truck-faces : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> List TruckFace
  truck-faces (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let axles = truck-axles bx
    in [truck-side segs ch pose d bx bx.xl, truck-side segs ch pose d bx bx.xr]
       & [truck-rear segs ch pose d bx, truck-roof segs ch pose d bx]
       & [truck-windshield segs ch pose d bx, truck-nose segs ch pose d bx]
       & side-tires segs ch pose d axles bx.xl 0
       & side-tires segs ch pose d axles bx.xr 0

Section: The Painter's Sort

 Farthest first, so the body occludes itself and its own far-side tires. The zig
 insertion-sorts sixteen faces in place and moves a key left only while the
 element before it is STRICTLY nearer, which is stable; this merges instead, for
 the reason Render's own sort gives at length -- an in-place insertion sort
 carried onto persistent lists gains a complexity class. Sixteen faces would
 survive it, but the answer is identical and there is no reason to keep two rules.

 It shares Render's `deeper-than` too, and for the same argument: two depths
 closer than half an f32 ulp are one number in the game and the port must not
 order them. The truck is where that is most likely to matter -- square-on to the
 eye, its two sides sit at exactly the same depth, and which one paints second
 decides nothing about the picture but everything about the command stream.

  face-rest : List TruckFace, Integer -> List TruckFace
  face-rest (ys) (j) =
    if j >= list-length ys then []
    else [list-at ys j] & face-rest ys (j + 1)

  merge-faces : List TruckFace, List TruckFace, Integer, Integer -> List TruckFace
  merge-faces (a) (b) (i) (j) =
    if i >= list-length a then face-rest b j
    else if j >= list-length b then face-rest a i
    else if deeper-than (list-at b j).fwd (list-at a i).fwd
      then [list-at b j] & merge-faces a b i (j + 1)
      else [list-at a i] & merge-faces a b (i + 1) j

  sort-faces : List TruckFace -> List TruckFace
  sort-faces (xs) =
    if list-length xs <= 1 then xs
    else merge-faces (sort-faces (list-take xs (list-length xs / 2))) (sort-faces (list-drop xs (list-length xs / 2))) 0 0

Section: Filling

 Near-clip, project, push -- the same pipeline a rail poly goes through, and it
 borrows GuardRail's `project-all` rather than restating it. push-poly's own
 under-three guard is what drops a polygon the clip reduced to a sliver.

  truck-fill : Integer, List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-fill (color) (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    push-poly color (project-all (clip-near ps near) cf view-w 0)

  draw-faces : List TruckFace, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-faces (fs) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length fs then []
    else (truck-fill (list-at fs i).color (list-at fs i).v cf view-w) & draw-faces fs cf view-w (i + 1)

Section: The Wedges And The Halo

 NEITHER GRADIENT SHAPE IS CLIPPED, AND BOTH ARE DROPPED WHOLE INSTEAD. The zig
 returns from drawWedge and from drawBrakeGlow the moment any corner is at or
 behind the near plane, rather than clipping it as a face is clipped. That is not
 an oversight to be improved on in the port: a radial gradient carries a centre
 and a radius in SCREEN space, and clipping the outline would leave the fill
 centred on a lamp that is no longer a corner of it. The truck is always at the
 far end of the chase, so the case is rare -- but it is the case where a clipped
 beam would be visibly wrong rather than merely absent.

  any-behind : List Vec3, Integer -> Boolean
  any-behind (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then False
    else if (list-at ps i).forward <= near then True
    else any-behind ps (i + 1)

  mid-vec : Vec3, Vec3 -> Vec3
  mid-vec (p) (q) =
    Vec3 { right = (p.right + q.right) / 2.0, forward = (p.forward + q.forward) / 2.0, height = (p.height + q.height) / 2.0 }

  pt-dist : ScreenPt, ScreenPt -> Real
  pt-dist (p) (c) =
    real-sqrt ((p.x - c.x) * (p.x - c.x) + (p.y - c.y) * (p.y - c.y))

 The gradient's radius is the FARTHEST corner, so the fill reaches every vertex of
 the shape it is filling and nothing is left flat at the tip.

  max-radius : List ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  max-radius (sp) (c) (i) =
    if i >= list-length sp then 0.0
    else real-max (pt-dist (list-at sp i) c) (max-radius sp c (i + 1))

 One wedge from one cab-front corner: near-bottom and near-top at the truck face
 (the slit), then far-top and far-bottom twenty metres down the road (fanned
 open). The lamp is the midpoint of the near edge and the glow is centred there.

  truck-wedge : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-wedge (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (src-x) (cf) (view-w) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 src-x (headlight-h - cone-near-half)
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 src-x (headlight-h + cone-near-half)
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d (bx.a2 + cone-length) src-x (cone-far-center + cone-far-half)
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d (bx.a2 + cone-length) src-x (cone-far-center - cone-far-half)
    in wedge-emit [p0, p1, p2, p3] cf view-w

  wedge-emit : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  wedge-emit (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    if any-behind ps 0 then [] else wedge-cmd ps cf view-w

  wedge-cmd : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  wedge-cmd (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sp = project-all ps cf view-w 0
    in let lamp = project (mid-vec (list-at ps 0) (list-at ps 1)) cf view-w
    in push-grad-poly beam-core beam-edge lamp.x lamp.y (max-radius sp lamp 0) sp

 Both beams, inset from each cab-front corner. They are pushed BEFORE the body so
 the opaque faces draw over them where they overlap -- mostly they spill onto the
 road ahead, which is what a headlight does.

  truck-wedges : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-wedges (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (cf) (view-w) =
    (truck-wedge segs ch pose d bx (bx.xl + headlight-inset) cf view-w)
    & (truck-wedge segs ch pose d bx (bx.xr - headlight-inset) cf view-w)

 The halo behind a brake-light panel: the panel's screen centre, a radius three
 times its own, and a sixteen-gon laid over it. It is a SCREEN-SPACE circle rather
 than a mapped one -- the light is a glow in the image, not a shape in the world.

  sum-x : List ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  sum-x (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).x + sum-x ps (i + 1)

  sum-y : List ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  sum-y (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).y + sum-y ps (i + 1)

  glow-pt : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> ScreenPt
  glow-pt (cx) (cy) (rad) (i) =
    let th = real-from-int i / real-from-int glow-sides * two-pi
    in ScreenPt { x = cx + rad * r-cos th, y = cy + rad * r-sin th }

  glow-circle : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  glow-circle (cx) (cy) (rad) (i) =
    if i >= glow-sides then []
    else [glow-pt cx cy rad i] & glow-circle cx cy rad (i + 1)

  brake-glow : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-glow (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    if any-behind panel 0 then [] else glow-cmd panel cf view-w

  glow-cmd : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  glow-cmd (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sp = project-all panel cf view-w 0
    in let cx = sum-x sp 0 / 4.0
    in let cy = sum-y sp 0 / 4.0
    in let rad = max-radius sp (ScreenPt { x = cx, y = cy }) 0 * 3.2
    in push-grad-poly glow-core glow-edge cx cy rad (glow-circle cx cy rad 0)

Section: The Brake Lights

 Two panels low on the trailer rear, drawn AFTER the body so they land on the face
 we chase rather than being sorted behind it. Each is a soft halo first and the
 bright core on top. They show only while the truck is ACTUALLY slowing, which is
 the flag Truck's motion half computes against the previous speed.

  brake-light : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-light (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (a0) (x0) (x1) (cf) (view-w) =
    let bl = trailer-bottom + 0.20 * (truck-height - trailer-bottom)
    in let bh = trailer-bottom + 0.50 * (truck-height - trailer-bottom)
    in let q0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x0 bl
    in let q1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x1 bl
    in let q2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x1 bh
    in let q3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x0 bh
    in emit-brake [q0, q1, q2, q3] cf view-w

  emit-brake : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-brake (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    (brake-glow panel cf view-w) & (truck-fill brake-color panel cf view-w)

  brake-lights : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-lights (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (cf) (view-w) =
    (brake-light segs ch pose d bx.a0 (bx.xl + 0.10 * truck-width) (bx.xl + 0.36 * truck-width) cf view-w)
    & (brake-light segs ch pose d bx.a0 (bx.xr - 0.36 * truck-width) (bx.xr - 0.10 * truck-width) cf view-w)

Section: The Whole Truck

 Beams under, body over, lights on top. `d` is the chain index Render's truck-at
 found the centre on, `center-along` is how far along that segment it sits, and
 `hw` is that segment's half width -- the centre line the box is built around.

  truck-draw-body : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Boolean, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-draw-body (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (center-along) (hw) (braking) (headlights) (cf) (view-w) =
    let bx = truck-box center-along hw
    in let beams = if headlights then truck-wedges segs ch pose d bx cf view-w else []
    in let body = draw-faces (sort-faces (truck-faces segs ch pose d bx)) cf view-w 0
    in let lights = if braking then brake-lights segs ch pose d bx cf view-w else []
    in beams & body & lights

Chapter: Grade

 The reusable grader: compare a flattened list of values against gold and say
 WHERE the first disagreement is.

 Every seam in this port reduces to the same shape -- a list of Reals, a list of
 Integers, a list of Booleans -- so one grader serves pond, the camera, and later
 the draw-command buffer, which is itself a flat list. Flattening at the seam is
 what makes that true.

 The judge is built out of nothing it judges: g-abs duplicates real-abs on
 purpose. DeviceMath's own accuracy test records why -- when its judge called the
 chapter under test, collapsing that chapter left every cell reading ok.

 A failure names the INDEX, not just BAD. `show` on a Real is refused by the zig
 plug, which needs a __real_to_text it does not have, so a Real cannot be printed
 to say what it was; an Integer index can, and it points at one value instead of
 at a whole seam. When real-to-int lands, this is the chapter that grows a
 scaled-integer dump and the tolerance argument goes away.

 We say:

Section: Judging Reals

  g-abs : Real -> Real
  g-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

  first-real-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-real-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-abs (list-at got i - list-at want i) > tol then i
    else first-real-diff got want tol (i + 1)

  grade-reals : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-reals (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-real-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Reals Relatively

 A RELATIVE tolerance, scaled by the larger of 1 and the wanted magnitude -- the
 form DeviceMath's own accuracy test uses. Computed values need it: the port
 works in Real (f64) while the game computes in f32, so a projected pixel and a
 focal length disagree in proportion to their size, not by a fixed amount. An
 absolute tolerance that passes a 0.9 duck height would wave through a 4-pixel
 error on a 685-pixel focal.

  g-max : Real, Real -> Real
  g-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  first-rel-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-rel-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else let w = list-at want i
      in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > tol * g-max 1.0 (g-abs w) then i
      else first-rel-diff got want tol (i + 1)

  grade-rel : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-rel (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-rel-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Cancelled Coordinates

 A MIXED tolerance: an absolute floor plus a relative term, which is the only form
 that fits a value produced by cancellation. Neither half works alone, and both
 failures were measured rather than guessed.

 IT IS NAMED FOR THE FAILURE MODE, NOT THE UNIT, and `grade-px` keeps its name
 because screen pixels were the first seam to need it and most of its callers are
 still projections. RenderCheck grades rider-frame METRES with it: `at` composes a
 point down a kilometre of chain and returns one a metre or two off the rider's
 nose, which is the same small-difference-of-two-large-numbers this gate exists
 for, in a different unit. The FLOOR is what changes with the unit -- a thousandth
 of a pixel on a canvas, an f32 ulp at the range a chain reaches -- and D5's rule
 is that it is calibrated per seam and never inherited.

 A RELATIVE gate fails near the horizon. A screen y is 300 minus a term of about
 the same size, so a y of 20.6 is a small difference of two large numbers and
 cancellation amplifies f32's relative error by the ratio between them -- fifteen
 times epsilon on the first cone tier of a near tree.

 An ABSOLUTE gate fails far off screen. Projecting a point 37 m to the side at the
 0.4 m near plane puts x at about -62929, where one f32 ulp is already 0.0075 px --
 seven times a thousandth-of-a-pixel floor, before any port is involved.

 The floor governs coordinates near zero and on the canvas; the relative term
 governs the far-off-screen ones the near plane throws out. At a hundred pixels the
 gate is about a thousandth of a pixel, which is the precision worth asserting.

  grade-px : Text, List Real, List Real, Real, Real -> Text
  grade-px (name) (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-px-diff got want atol rtol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

  first-px-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-px-diff (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else let w = list-at want i
      in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > atol + rtol * g-abs w then i
      else first-px-diff got want atol rtol (i + 1)

Section: Judging Integers

  first-int-diff : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  first-int-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if list-at got i /= list-at want i then i
    else first-int-diff got want (i + 1)

  grade-ints : Text, List Integer, List Integer -> Text
  grade-ints (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-int-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i & " got " & show (list-at got i) & " want " & show (list-at want i)

Section: Judging Booleans

  bool-eq : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  bool-eq (a) (b) = if a then b else if b then False else True

  first-bool-diff : List Boolean, List Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  first-bool-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if bool-eq (list-at got i) (list-at want i) then first-bool-diff got want (i + 1)
    else i

  grade-bools : Text, List Boolean, List Boolean -> Text
  grade-bools (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-bool-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Chapter: TruckBodyGold

 GENERATED by harness/gen_gold.py from probe/probe_truck_body.zig.
 Every value here came from importing the real game module unmodified.
 Do not edit: the next harness run overwrites this file.

 We say:

Section: Gold

  g-tb-tag : List Integer
